00:22:09,700 --> 00:22:11,230
Ise shrimp.

518
00:22:11,230 --> 00:22:12,800
- This is shrimp?
- This is shrimp.

519
00:22:13,200 --> 00:22:15,710
It looks just like crayfish.

520
00:22:16,240 --> 00:22:17,370
It's looking at me.

521
00:22:17,770 --> 00:22:19,040
- This one is looking at you.
- Let's try it.

522
00:22:19,610 --> 00:22:21,450
- This is shrimp? This?
- It's shrimp.

523
00:22:21,450 --> 00:22:22,980
Why didn't I know about this?

524
00:22:22,980 --> 00:22:25,880
(It's so big, it's hard to believe it's shrimp.)

525
00:22:26,980 --> 00:22:28,050
It doesn't seem like shrimp.

526
00:22:28,050 --> 00:22:29,890
- It's like lobster.
- This is lobster.

527
00:22:31,120 --> 00:22:32,460
Hello, everyone.

528
00:22:33,490 --> 00:22:34,890
Hello, everyone.

529
00:22:36,460 --> 00:22:37,930
I am here.

530
00:22:37,930 --> 00:22:39,130
(Japanese presenter, I am here.)

531
00:22:39,130 --> 00:22:40,960
What I'll present to you today...

532
00:22:41,470 --> 00:22:44,100
is Japan's most famous shrimp, the Ise shrimp.

533
00:22:45,140 --> 00:22:47,600
A lot of Ise shrimp is caught in the coastal waters...

534
00:22:47,600 --> 00:22:49,310
of the Pacific Ocean near Japan.

535
00:22:49,910 --> 00:22:52,610
They're 20cm to 40cm on average.

536
00:22:52,980 --> 00:22:54,610
They don't have big claws,

537
00:22:54,750 --> 00:22:58,010
and they have long antennae and hard shells.

538
00:22:59,650 --> 00:23:02,690
(A premium ingredient with a chewy texture and sweet flavour)

539
00:23:03,120 --> 00:23:05,020
Doesn't Ise shrimp look delicious?

540
00:23:05,020 --> 00:23:06,790
(Ise shrimp, surprising size and texture)

541
00:23:09,230 --> 00:23:10,360
Should I have a bite?

542
00:23:18,240 --> 00:23:19,300
Is it good?

543
00:23:21,070 --> 00:23:22,340
It's a completely new flavour.

544
00:23:22,440 --> 00:23:23,440
- Really?
- Yes.

545
00:23:23,440 --> 00:23:25,980
You've had a lot in Central and South America.

546
00:23:26,380 --> 00:23:27,380
But those were all grilled.

547
00:23:27,380 --> 00:23:30,980
But this shrimp.... The texture is very unique.

548
00:23:31,080 --> 00:23:32,080
Really?

549
00:23:33,550 --> 00:23:36,090
- It's good. It's really good.
- It's like it has added sugar.

550
00:23:37,050 --> 00:23:40,720
(A soft but chewy texture of the tender flesh)

551
00:23:50,300 --> 00:23:51,740
(Nodding)

552
00:23:52,500 --> 00:23:54,470
It feels bouncy when you chew it.

553
00:23:55,640 --> 00:23:57,710
It really doesn't feel like shrimp.

554
00:23:58,010 --> 00:23:59,840
- It feels like lobster sashimi.
- Exactly.

555
00:24:01,180 --> 00:24:02,550
(They can't stop reaching for the chewy sweetness...)

556
00:24:02,550 --> 00:24:04,210
(that feels like lobster.)

557
00:24:05,520 --> 00:24:06,650
This is excellent.

558
00:24:08,490 --> 00:24:09,550
(The health benefits of Ise shrimp)

559
00:24:09,550 --> 00:24:10,790
(Arginine revitalises the body, vitamin E improves circulation,)

560
00:24:10,790 --> 00:24:11,790
(taurine stimulates brain development,)

561
00:24:11,790 --> 00:24:12,860
(astaxanthin for anti-ageing)

562
00:24:12,860 --> 00:24:15,490
The market price of this...

563
00:24:15,590 --> 00:24:17,090
is 6,760 yen.

564
00:24:17,090 --> 00:24:19,260
- So...
- About 68 dollars?

565
00:24:19,260 --> 00:24:20,900
- Around 70 dollars?
- Yes.

566
00:24:20,900 --> 00:24:23,600
But in Korea, it'd be around 150 dollars.

567
00:24:23,930 --> 00:24:25,570
We have this in Korea?

568
00:24:25,570 --> 00:24:26,640
I'm sure they import some.

569
00:24:26,640 --> 00:24:28,310
Or if you want to eat something around this size,

570
00:24:28,310 --> 00:24:29,510
it'd be around 150 dollars.

571
00:24:29,510 --> 00:24:31,470
But you can eat this for 70 dollars.

572
00:24:31,470 --> 00:24:32,740
Because it's fresh off the sea.

573
00:24:34,610 --> 00:24:37,280
Right, I am curious about the total price.

574
00:24:37,280 --> 00:24:39,320
Because in Vietnam, everything was very cheap.

575
00:24:39,550 --> 00:24:40,650
Vietnam is cheaper.

576
00:24:40,650 --> 00:24:42,350
- You can't compare to Vietnam.
- Vietnam is really...

577
00:24:42,350 --> 00:24:44,050
- Right.
- It was really...

578
00:24:44,250 --> 00:24:45,960
Nam Gil, this outfit set was five dollars.

579
00:24:45,960 --> 00:24:47,820
(While they're on the topic,)

580
00:24:47,820 --> 00:24:49,130
(Joon shows off his Da Nang travel outfit to Nam Gil.)

581
00:24:49,930 --> 00:24:51,260
Even the person who made this must be surprised.

582
00:24:51,260 --> 00:24:53,200
You must be the first person to wear it this much.

583
00:24:56,100 --> 00:24:58,400
Would you like to have this as miso soup?

584
00:24:59,100 --> 00:25:00,640
(After the staff and Hyun Moo talk)

585
00:25:01,770 --> 00:25:03,070
- What's going on?
- They're going to take this,

586
00:25:03,070 --> 00:25:05,540
cut it into three, and make something with it.

587
00:25:05,710 --> 00:25:06,840
To finish the meal.

588
00:25:06,840 --> 00:25:08,380
Like Korean maeuntang?

589
00:25:08,910 --> 00:25:10,150
It's not maeuntang.

590
00:25:10,910 --> 00:25:15,090
(How will the Ise shrimp they took be transformed?)

591
00:25:15,620 --> 00:25:17,420
This really is a local flavour.

592
00:25:18,690 --> 00:25:21,190
You know, when we go to Jeju Island,

593
00:25:21,190 --> 00:25:23,960
the seafood haenyeos catch is tasty, right?

594
00:25:23,960 --> 00:25:24,960
- It is.
- Right.

595
00:25:24,960 --> 00:25:26,730
- It's like that.
- Yes.

596
00:25:26,730 --> 00:25:28,330
The ingredients are next level.

597
00:25:29,130 --> 00:25:32,270
Since you usually go to big cities for fan meet-ups,

598
00:25:32,270 --> 00:25:34,700
- you haven't been in small cities.
- Right.

599
00:25:34,700 --> 00:25:35,740
Right?

600
00:25:36,310 --> 00:25:38,040
So I like this kind of thing a lot more.

601
00:25:38,040 --> 00:25:39,640
If you like motorcycles,

602
00:25:39,640 --> 00:25:42,480
it must be amazing to ride along the coast.

603
00:25:42,480 --> 00:25:44,080
Stop by a place like this, have a bite to eat.

604
00:25:46,680 --> 00:25:48,550
I love visiting places like this.

605
00:25:49,720 --> 00:25:53,760
(A seafood restaurant by the road you'd want to visit...)

606
00:25:54,560 --> 00:25:56,490
(when you're feeling hungry...)

607
00:25:56,490 --> 00:25:59,230
(after a refreshing drive along the coast)

608
00:25:59,830 --> 00:26:01,360
When I travel,

609
00:26:01,360 --> 00:26:03,970
I want to go see how the local people live.

610
00:26:03,970 --> 00:26:05,670
Like how you go to restaurants.

611
00:26:06,070 --> 00:26:09,240
I want to explore the alleys and the places people live.

612
00:26:09,640 --> 00:26:10,670
I get it.

613
00:26:10,870 --> 00:26:14,240
I like experiencing the atmosphere of that country.

614
00:26:14,240 --> 00:26:15,580
I get it. It's so nice.

615
00:26:16,280 --> 00:26:17,310
Yes.

616
00:26:18,250 --> 00:26:19,350
What is this?

617
00:26:19,350 --> 00:26:21,150
(The Ise shrimp that was taken away is back.)

618
00:26:21,150 --> 00:26:23,220
- You were right. It's maeuntang.
- It's maeuntang.

619
00:26:23,620 --> 00:26:25,220
- Joon was right.
- Yes.

620
00:26:25,220 --> 00:26:26,690
- It's Japanese maeuntang.
- Here we go.

621
00:26:26,690 --> 00:26:28,390
(The Ise shrimp came back as maeuntang.)

622
00:26:28,860 --> 00:26:30,190
In this seaweed soup...

623
00:26:30,190 --> 00:26:31,590
(The clear seaweed soup that was served with the meal)

624
00:26:32,030 --> 00:26:33,400
And the remaining flesh, right?

625
00:26:33,400 --> 00:26:34,830
(The Ise shrimp was cooked inside.)

626
00:26:35,400 --> 00:26:36,970
(Ise shrimp miso shiru)

627
00:26:36,970 --> 00:26:38,840
(The final dish of the all-in-one Tokushima seafood meal)

628
00:26:52,880 --> 00:26:54,150
Oh, that broth is nice.

629
00:27:01,360 --> 00:27:02,360
(Oh, my.)

630
00:27:02,360 --> 00:27:03,960
- Isn't the broth nice?
- Even though I didn't drink...

631
00:27:05,630 --> 00:27:06,800
It's not a maeuntang.

632
00:27:06,800 --> 00:27:08,130
It's a soybean paste base.

633
00:27:08,130 --> 00:27:09,430
- Miso.
- Yes.

634
00:27:09,430 --> 00:27:10,670
You're right. Miso soup.

635
00:27:10,670 --> 00:27:13,640
(The three brothers are in love with the Ise shrimp miso soup.)

636
00:27:14,270 --> 00:27:15,440
- It's good.
- This is nice.

637
00:27:16,540 --> 00:27:18,110
I like this more than the shrimp.

638
00:27:23,880 --> 00:27:24,910
(Exclaiming)

639
00:27:26,320 --> 00:27:29,950
This course menu is similar to the Korean one.

640
00:27:29,950 --> 00:27:31,990
- Right. If we just get maeuntang...
- As it keeps coming...

641
00:27:36,660 --> 00:27:39,730
What did you think of the first all-in-one Tokushima meal?

642
00:27:39,900 --> 00:27:41,160
It was satisfying.

643
00:27:41,160 --> 00:27:42,530
- Completely satisfying?
- Completely satisfying.

644
00:27:42,770 --> 00:27:45,270
Also, the sea bream with a broken back that you talked about...

645
00:27:45,270 --> 00:27:46,340
The sea bream with a broken back.

646
00:27:46,900 --> 00:27:49,070
When you're travelling, do you like...

647
00:27:49,470 --> 00:27:51,310
things like art?

648
00:27:51,640 --> 00:27:53,280
Yes. I don't know much about it,

649
00:27:53,280 --> 00:27:54,940
but I enjoy looking at it.

650
00:27:55,080 --> 00:27:56,980
- What about you?
- I don't know much but I like it.

651
00:27:57,450 --> 00:27:58,520
- You like it?
- Do you know art?

652
00:27:59,580 --> 00:28:01,350
- I don't know much about it.
- I know nothing about it.

653
00:28:01,350 --> 00:28:03,190
- Me neither.
- I don't know much either.

654
00:28:03,620 --> 00:28:04,920
- But...
- Art?

655
00:28:04,990 --> 00:28:07,590
when you go to Paris, you just go to the Louvre.

656
00:28:07,590 --> 00:28:09,230
- That's right.
- Even if you don't know art.

657
00:28:09,760 --> 00:28:11,960
Here, you'll be really surprised...

658
00:28:11,960 --> 00:28:13,000
because...

659
00:28:13,000 --> 00:28:14,000
(They're puzzled by the increasingly long explanation.)

660
00:28:14,260 --> 00:28:15,630
- I'll tell you when we're there.
- Okay.

661
00:28:15,770 --> 00:28:16,830
Let's go quickly.

662
00:28:16,830 --> 00:28:18,330
- Are we going there now?
- We can go there.

663
00:28:18,500 --> 00:28:19,570
I had a good meal.

664
00:28:19,570 --> 00:28:20,870
- It was great.
- I had a good meal.

665
00:28:25,580 --> 00:28:26,640
There was such a place?

666
00:28:26,640 --> 00:28:28,750
Wait, we were here before, right?

667
00:28:28,750 --> 00:28:31,280
- It's the same place.
- It looks different now.

668
00:28:31,780 --> 00:28:35,190
How did they make this?

669
00:28:37,090 --> 00:28:38,220
What ramyeon do you like?

670
00:28:38,760 --> 00:28:39,960
Ramyeon?

671
00:28:40,220 --> 00:28:41,960
I had N ramyeon even yesterday.

672
00:28:43,030 --> 00:28:44,130
At night.

673
00:28:44,130 --> 00:28:45,360
So you like that one.

674
00:28:45,500 --> 00:28:46,560
What do you like?

675
00:28:46,730 --> 00:28:47,860
I like Y ramyeon.

676
00:28:47,860 --> 00:28:49,100
What ramyeon do you like, Joon?

677
00:28:49,100 --> 00:28:51,370
- Spicy J ramyeon.
- That's tasty.

678
00:28:51,370 --> 00:28:53,600
- Spicy J ramyeon tastes good.
- It's amazing.

679
00:28:53,840 --> 00:28:56,170
I'm talking about this with guys. How nice.

680
00:28:56,170 --> 00:28:57,310
This is crazy.

681
00:28:57,540 --> 00:28:59,510
The person who invented ramyeon should get a Nobel Peace Prize.

682
00:29:08,520 --> 00:29:09,620
We're here.

683
00:29:10,290 --> 00:29:11,890
It looks like a cave.

684
00:29:11,890 --> 00:29:13,120
Would it be a cave?

685
00:29:13,620 --> 00:29:15,830
Isn't it inside the mountain?

686
00:29:15,990 --> 00:29:17,490
I've never seen anything like this.

687
00:29:18,430 --> 00:29:24,100
(Binbiya Restaurant, Otsuka Museum of Art)

688
00:29:25,800 --> 00:29:28,300
Okay, now this place...

689
00:29:28,670 --> 00:29:29,810
Where are we?

690
00:29:30,340 --> 00:29:32,340
Otsuka Museum of Art.

691
00:29:32,640 --> 00:29:34,910
- Otsuka Museum of Art.
- It's an art museum.

692
00:29:37,780 --> 00:29:39,220
It wouldn't be fun to look around ourselves...

693
00:29:39,220 --> 00:29:40,850
- since we don't know much.
- That's right.

694
00:29:40,850 --> 00:29:42,920
So I asked a friend who I came here with last time...

695
00:29:42,920 --> 00:29:44,920
to come.

696
00:29:45,260 --> 00:29:46,320
- A friend?
- It's...

697
00:29:46,460 --> 00:29:47,790
Docent Lee Chang Yong.

698
00:29:47,790 --> 00:29:48,830
What?

699
00:29:49,160 --> 00:29:50,890
What? Hello.

700
00:29:51,560 --> 00:29:53,800
(I'll prove that art isn't boring!)

701
00:29:54,130 --> 00:29:56,400
(A docent who worked at the Orsay Museum, the Louvre,)

702
00:29:56,400 --> 00:29:58,670
(and the Vatican Museums.)

703
00:29:58,770 --> 00:30:02,270
(The Man who Reads Paintings who conquered the great museums)

704
00:30:02,910 --> 00:30:05,340
(Hyun Moo's long time art and museum buddy)

705
00:30:05,710 --> 00:30:07,040
Did he come here for this?

706
00:30:07,180 --> 00:30:09,550
- I invited him.
- What's with the cast?

707
00:30:09,850 --> 00:30:11,710
It's become a blockbuster.

708
00:30:11,710 --> 00:30:14,880
Out of my schedule for the second half of the year,

709
00:30:14,880 --> 00:30:16,490
only today was free.

710
00:30:16,590 --> 00:30:17,890
I know that. I'm sorry.

711
00:30:17,890 --> 00:30:19,860
Today is my wedding anniversary.

712
00:30:19,860 --> 00:30:20,960
Goodness.

713
00:30:21,060 --> 00:30:23,460
- I'm really sorry.
- So I left today open.

714
00:30:23,460 --> 00:30:26,460
- He just had to pick that day.
- I couldn't help it.

715
00:30:26,460 --> 00:30:29,130
He's the person who told me about this art museum.

716
00:30:29,130 --> 00:30:31,770
- Really?
- We came here on an art tour.

717
00:30:31,770 --> 00:30:33,270
- I...
- It's an honour.

718
00:30:33,270 --> 00:30:34,400
- Let's go.
- Let's go.

719
00:30:34,400 --> 00:30:35,440
- Let's go.
- This way.

720
00:30:36,010 --> 00:30:37,470
We came to an art museum.

721
00:30:37,470 --> 00:30:39,580
Chang Yong will tell you later,

722
00:30:39,580 --> 00:30:41,040
but this museum...

723
00:30:41,280 --> 00:30:44,410
has artworks from Leonardo da Vinci and many others...

724
00:30:45,450 --> 00:30:47,720
that look identical to the real ones.

725
00:30:47,820 --> 00:30:50,220
(A museum that recreates artworks at their original size)

726
00:30:50,420 --> 00:30:53,660
It's like visiting every museum and art gallery in the world.

727
00:30:55,190 --> 00:30:58,030
(An all-in-one art museum)

728
00:30:58,660 --> 00:31:00,200
You can even touch them.

729
00:31:00,360 --> 00:31:01,700
- Really?
- It's amazing.

730
00:31:01,700 --> 00:31:02,830
I'm really looking forward to it.

731
00:31:04,700 --> 00:31:06,200
Let's enter the first gallery.

732
00:31:10,440 --> 00:31:12,340
You'll see why this place is amazing.

733
00:31:13,440 --> 00:31:15,980
This is...

734
00:31:16,410 --> 00:31:18,920
It's not the massive scale that amazes you.

735
00:31:19,550 --> 00:31:21,380
- It looks exactly the same.
- It looks the same.

736
00:31:21,580 --> 00:31:22,650
All of this?

737
00:31:26,990 --> 00:31:30,090
("The Creation of Adam," capturing the moment life is given)

738
00:31:30,230 --> 00:31:33,100
(Michelangelo's immortal masterpiece)

739
00:31:34,100 --> 00:31:35,870
(The Vatican, where white smoke rises...)

740
00:31:35,870 --> 00:31:39,040
(with the election of every new pope)

741
00:31:40,800 --> 00:31:42,640
(The Sistine Chapel, where Michelangelo's masterpieces...)

742
00:31:42,640 --> 00:31:43,670
(come to life)

743
00:31:43,770 --> 00:31:47,310
(You can see works from the Vatican in Tokushima.)

744
00:31:47,780 --> 00:31:52,320
(Vatican City's Sistine Chapel)

745
00:31:52,450 --> 00:31:54,720
This is... How grand it is.

746
00:31:55,490 --> 00:31:57,190
It's frighteningly huge.

747
00:31:58,120 --> 00:32:00,060
It's exactly the same as the original one.

748
00:32:00,520 --> 00:32:01,760
Even the size, right?

749
00:32:01,760 --> 00:32:02,860
- Right.
- Even the colour.

750
00:32:03,160 --> 00:32:05,060
(Exclaiming)

751
00:32:05,560 --> 00:32:06,800
It's overwhelming.

752
00:32:07,130 --> 00:32:09,130
It's like I'm being swallowed up by this.

753
00:32:12,740 --> 00:32:16,140
The artwork on the ceiling is the ceiling fresco...

754
00:32:16,140 --> 00:32:18,740
of the Sistine Chapel, commonly known as "The Creation."

755
00:32:19,380 --> 00:32:20,510
("The Sistine Chapel Ceiling")

756
00:32:20,510 --> 00:32:21,640
(Michelangelo's masterpiece depicting nine scenes from Genesis,)

757
00:32:21,640 --> 00:32:24,280
(from the creation of the world to the story of Noah)

758
00:32:24,410 --> 00:32:26,480
This is amazing.

759
00:32:27,250 --> 00:32:30,090
It's at a height of 20m,

760
00:32:31,620 --> 00:32:33,790
and the curved ceiling was reproduced as well.

761
00:32:33,790 --> 00:32:35,360
(The illusion of a curved ceiling was recreated faithfully.)

762
00:32:36,130 --> 00:32:38,730
Even "The Last Judgment" at the front was recreated...

763
00:32:40,100 --> 00:32:42,300
at its original size, 13m by 12m.

764
00:32:42,300 --> 00:32:43,630
How did they do that?

765
00:32:43,630 --> 00:32:45,400
("The Last Judgment")

766
00:32:45,400 --> 00:32:46,870
(A work depicting Christ's return to judge humanity)

767
00:32:53,040 --> 00:32:56,310
I'm really getting goosebumps from looking at this.

768
00:32:58,010 --> 00:32:59,150
Amazing.

769
00:33:06,260 --> 00:33:07,490
Goodness, this...

770
00:33:07,490 --> 00:33:10,030
(Nam Gil follows the story as if mesmerized.)

771
00:33:10,030 --> 00:33:13,230
(Exclaiming)

772
00:33:14,630 --> 00:33:15,730
My neck hurts.

773
00:33:17,270 --> 00:33:20,240
Michelangelo painted this entire work by himself.

774
00:33:20,240 --> 00:33:22,710
- Michelangelo, when he was young.
- He worked on it alone...

775
00:33:22,710 --> 00:33:25,240
from the age of 33 to 37. For 4 years and 6 months.

776
00:33:25,240 --> 00:33:26,540
That's really amazing.

777
00:33:26,680 --> 00:33:29,650
He even didn't paint straight on the wall.

778
00:33:29,910 --> 00:33:31,810
In the past, frescoes were painted...

779
00:33:32,250 --> 00:33:33,620
by first applying a layer of wet plaster.

780
00:33:33,780 --> 00:33:36,590
Then, you had to sketch...

781
00:33:36,820 --> 00:33:38,350
- and paint quickly...
- Goodness.

782
00:33:38,350 --> 00:33:39,520
before the plaster dried.

783
00:33:39,760 --> 00:33:41,290
What happens is,

784
00:33:41,720 --> 00:33:43,690
as the plaster hardens,

785
00:33:43,690 --> 00:33:45,330
the pigments are absorbed into the wall.

786
00:33:45,500 --> 00:33:47,600
Then the wall itself becomes the painting.

787
00:33:47,600 --> 00:33:49,730
(Fresco, a mural painting technique...)

788
00:33:49,730 --> 00:33:51,800
(that uses water-based pigments on wet plaster before it dries)

789
00:33:52,340 --> 00:33:53,670
This has clear advantages and disadvantages.

790
00:33:53,670 --> 00:33:56,310
The advantage is that the painting lasts long.

791
00:33:56,610 --> 00:33:57,740
The colours don't change.

792
00:33:57,740 --> 00:33:59,780
But the disadvantage. What do you think it is?

793
00:34:02,880 --> 00:34:04,050
It can't be amended?

794
00:34:04,050 --> 00:34:05,150
That's exactly right.

795
00:34:05,280 --> 00:34:06,620
(Exclaiming)

796
00:34:06,820 --> 00:34:08,380
The wall hardens.

797
00:34:08,380 --> 00:34:09,650
So you can't make amendments.

798
00:34:09,650 --> 00:34:13,160
So these paintings were painted without corrections.

799
00:34:13,160 --> 00:34:15,630
He painted this for 4 years and 6 months,

800
00:34:15,630 --> 00:34:17,060
but in the first six months, he failed.

801
00:34:17,060 --> 00:34:18,330
Goodness.

802
00:34:18,330 --> 00:34:20,030
It was Michelangelo's...

803
00:34:20,200 --> 00:34:22,130
first time painting as a painter.

804
00:34:22,330 --> 00:34:24,700
So he made mistakes.

805
00:34:25,200 --> 00:34:26,970
This is what happened.

806
00:34:27,340 --> 00:34:30,510
The nine pieces in the middle tell the main story.

807
00:34:30,870 --> 00:34:33,280
In terms of the story, it follows Genesis.

808
00:34:34,480 --> 00:34:36,410
Light and darkness are separated.

809
00:34:36,410 --> 00:34:37,480
(Separating light from darkness)

810
00:34:37,480 --> 00:34:38,850
The sun and the moon were created.

811
00:34:39,680 --> 00:34:41,580
Adam and Eve were created.

812
00:34:41,580 --> 00:34:44,390
They were banished from Eden, then all the way to Noah's story.

813
00:34:44,390 --> 00:34:46,920
In chronological order, the story unfolds like this.

814
00:34:47,460 --> 00:34:48,720
But he painted it in reverse order.

815
00:34:48,720 --> 00:34:49,960
(Painting order, Biblical story order)

816
00:34:49,960 --> 00:34:52,090
So while he worked from that side to this side,

817
00:34:52,330 --> 00:34:56,170
the painting somehow changed.

818
00:34:57,300 --> 00:34:58,370
What's different?

819
00:35:00,440 --> 00:35:02,340
- I don't think you told me.
- Just think of it simply.

820
00:35:02,340 --> 00:35:03,470
I didn't tell you before.

821
00:35:03,670 --> 00:35:05,170
(Thinking)

822
00:35:05,170 --> 00:35:06,510
Are there more people?

823
00:35:06,510 --> 00:35:08,980
- That's correct.
- Goodness.

824
00:35:08,980 --> 00:35:10,050
That's correct.

825
00:35:10,050 --> 00:35:11,510
Goodness.

826
00:35:11,510 --> 00:35:12,550
Awesome.

827
00:35:12,680 --> 00:35:13,820
That's exactly right.

828
00:35:14,050 --> 00:35:15,520
- There are more people?
- If you look at that,

829
00:35:15,750 --> 00:35:17,490
he painted this...

830
00:35:17,490 --> 00:35:19,420
from a scaffolding like this.

831
00:35:19,420 --> 00:35:20,760
He painted it from a close distance.

832
00:35:20,760 --> 00:35:22,020
He could see the person clearly.

833
00:35:22,360 --> 00:35:24,960
But from 20m below, could you see them?

834
00:35:25,260 --> 00:35:27,400
- You can't see them.
- Right.

835
00:35:27,500 --> 00:35:29,730
So the painting becomes simpler toward the end.

836
00:35:29,730 --> 00:35:31,030
(60 people, 8 people, 6 people, 3 people, 2 people, 1 person)

837
00:35:31,030 --> 00:35:33,470
- Since he'd never painted before.
- He was only human.

838
00:35:33,470 --> 00:35:34,770
He realised that as he went on.

839
00:35:34,770 --> 00:35:36,840
It was because he never looked at it from here.

840
00:35:36,840 --> 00:35:38,310
- That's right.
- He couldn't see it so well.

841
00:35:38,310 --> 00:35:40,480
As for the piece in front,

842
00:35:40,880 --> 00:35:43,080
Michelangelo painted it in his 60s.

843
00:35:43,080 --> 00:35:46,550
He painted it for 5 years, from the age of 61 to 66.

844
00:35:46,550 --> 00:35:47,750
Goodness.

845
00:35:47,750 --> 00:35:48,780
(He painted humanity's end 24 years after he painted its beginning.)

846
00:35:53,120 --> 00:35:55,660
It's really elaborate.

847
00:35:59,030 --> 00:36:01,930
There's a story behind this one.

848
00:36:02,300 --> 00:36:05,070
There was a cardinal named Cesena at the Vatican...

849
00:36:05,070 --> 00:36:07,040
who would always come and criticize Michelangelo.

850
00:36:07,400 --> 00:36:09,140
They're all naked.

851
00:36:09,210 --> 00:36:10,310
It's indecent.

852
00:36:10,310 --> 00:36:12,210
This painting belongs to a bathhouse,

853
00:36:13,080 --> 00:36:14,710
not the Vatican.

854
00:36:14,710 --> 00:36:16,910
He criticized it every day,

855
00:36:16,910 --> 00:36:18,210
so this is what Michelangelo did.

856
00:36:18,410 --> 00:36:19,850
At the bottom right,

857
00:36:20,120 --> 00:36:22,690
he drew the cardinal as Minos, the judge of the underworld.

858
00:36:23,520 --> 00:36:25,760
He painted the cardinal as a demon of the underworld?

859
00:36:25,760 --> 00:36:26,820
Which one is it?

860
00:36:26,820 --> 00:36:28,260
The person on the very right.

861
00:36:28,260 --> 00:36:30,290
Goodness.

862
00:36:30,530 --> 00:36:31,960
So this is Cardinal Mesena?

863
00:36:31,960 --> 00:36:33,560
Yes. This is Cardinal Cesena.

864
00:36:33,560 --> 00:36:34,630
Cesena.

865
00:36:34,730 --> 00:36:36,430
He has unique ears...

866
00:36:36,430 --> 00:36:38,330
that look like a donkey's.

867
00:36:38,330 --> 00:36:40,100
That means he's foolish.

868
00:36:40,100 --> 00:36:41,800
(Donkeys symbolize foolishness.)

869
00:36:42,140 --> 00:36:43,970
There's another unique feature.

870
00:36:44,340 --> 00:36:45,980
(Unique feature?)

871
00:36:46,040 --> 00:36:47,040
Right. It's being bitten.

872
00:36:47,040 --> 00:36:49,010
Right. It's being bitten. Why was it painted like that?

873
00:36:49,680 --> 00:36:50,810
What's being bitten?

874
00:36:51,550 --> 00:36:52,780
That part is being bitten.

875
00:36:53,220 --> 00:36:55,520
- A snake is wrapped...
- Goodness.

876
00:36:55,520 --> 00:36:58,890
around the cardinal's body, biting his genitals.

877
00:36:58,890 --> 00:37:00,090
Why was it painted like that?

878
00:37:00,320 --> 00:37:02,090
- To castrate him?
- To castrate him.

879
00:37:02,560 --> 00:37:04,830
- Really?
- At the time, Cardinal Cesena...

880
00:37:04,830 --> 00:37:06,060
was a priest.

881
00:37:06,100 --> 00:37:09,070
Yet, he had an illegitimate child.

882
00:37:12,030 --> 00:37:14,570
So it's saying, "I know what you've been up to."

883
00:37:14,570 --> 00:37:17,110
- He got back at him.
- He got back at him.

884
00:37:17,210 --> 00:37:19,980
(Cardinal Cesena got quite the payback.)

885
00:37:19,980 --> 00:37:22,750
(He's being tormented forever.)

886
00:37:23,080 --> 00:37:24,310
We can take pictures of this, right?

887
00:37:24,310 --> 00:37:25,680
- Do it freely.
- You can touch the paintings too.

888
00:37:25,680 --> 00:37:27,550
- You can take pictures.
- Take pictures and send them to me.

889
00:37:27,550 --> 00:37:29,720
Okay. This is crazy.

890
00:37:29,720 --> 00:37:31,920
When you enter the Sistine Chapel,

891
00:37:31,920 --> 00:37:34,720
the guards only say two things.

892
00:37:34,720 --> 00:37:36,730
- What do they say?
- "Silence."

893
00:37:37,060 --> 00:37:38,190
- Silence.
- "No photo."

894
00:37:38,190 --> 00:37:40,460
- No photo, silence.
- No photo.

895
00:37:40,460 --> 00:37:42,530
(It's okay to take photos in Otsuka Museum of Art.)

896
00:37:45,970 --> 00:37:48,170
(World-famous masterpieces to take in with your eyes...)

897
00:37:48,170 --> 00:37:49,340
(and keep in photographs)

898
00:37:52,040 --> 00:37:53,680
This is so interesting.

899
00:37:54,140 --> 00:37:56,210
Since he explains so well,

900
00:37:56,210 --> 00:37:58,210
- It's fun, isn't it?
- it's like watching a movie.

901
00:37:58,210 --> 00:38:00,150
- Goodness.
- This...

902
00:38:01,250 --> 00:38:03,220
This is my favourite place.

903
00:38:04,050 --> 00:38:05,760
We didn't come here before.

904
00:38:05,760 --> 00:38:06,860
- We did.
- Really?

905
00:38:06,860 --> 00:38:07,960
I'm sorry.

906
00:38:08,220 --> 00:38:10,130
Wait.

907
00:38:10,130 --> 00:38:11,130
Do you know what it is?

908
00:38:11,260 --> 00:38:12,600
Right.

909
00:38:13,060 --> 00:38:15,060
- Goodness.
- "The Fiery Priest."

910
00:38:15,060 --> 00:38:16,530
This is so...

911
00:38:17,530 --> 00:38:18,730
"The Fiery Priest."

912
00:38:18,730 --> 00:38:20,670
That's right. Because of that...

913
00:38:20,670 --> 00:38:22,340
He's "The Fiery Priest."

914
00:38:23,210 --> 00:38:25,680
- It gave me chills.
- That's right.

915
00:38:28,210 --> 00:38:30,150
I can guarantee you,

916
00:38:30,150 --> 00:38:31,780
even among art history majors,

917
00:38:31,780 --> 00:38:35,020
fewer than ten percent have actually seen...

918
00:38:35,020 --> 00:38:36,750
this Giotto masterpiece in person.

919
00:38:38,390 --> 00:38:40,390
(A masterpiece by Giotto di Bondone,)

920
00:38:40,390 --> 00:38:41,760
(a pioneer of Renaissance art)

921
00:38:41,760 --> 00:38:42,760
Where is the original piece?

922
00:38:42,760 --> 00:38:44,390
Padua in northern Italy.

923
00:38:44,390 --> 00:38:46,000
Who'd go all the way there to Padua in Italy?

924
00:38:46,000 --> 00:38:47,000
Not many.

925
00:38:47,000 --> 00:38:48,560
- This is amazing.
- Who'd go all the way there?

926
00:38:48,560 --> 00:38:49,670
This is beautiful.

927
00:38:49,670 --> 00:38:52,540
(Padua, Italy's Scrovegni Chapel)

928
00:38:56,670 --> 00:38:58,310
- The Fiery Priest.
- Yes.

929
00:38:58,310 --> 00:39:00,340
- It really is the Fiery Priest.
- In the name of...

930
00:39:00,340 --> 00:39:01,710
- He's the real one.
- He's the Fiery Priest.

931
00:39:03,410 --> 00:39:05,820
What stands out to you first?

932
00:39:06,580 --> 00:39:07,880
- Blue?
- In this space.

933
00:39:07,880 --> 00:39:10,150
Blue. Joon is doing great today.

934
00:39:11,290 --> 00:39:13,090
The ceiling up there. The blue ceiling.

935
00:39:13,090 --> 00:39:14,560
That blue that looks like it's about to overflow.

936
00:39:16,290 --> 00:39:17,460
It's so nice.

937
00:39:20,230 --> 00:39:23,670
(In Renaissance period, blue represents divinity.)

938
00:39:25,630 --> 00:39:27,570
It feels like I'm in paradise.

939
00:39:27,570 --> 00:39:29,310
Really. Looking at all the blue...

940
00:39:29,540 --> 00:39:32,240
(Nam Gil can't keep his mouth shut all day.)

941
00:39:32,480 --> 00:39:35,850
This chapel was built in the early 14th century.

942
00:39:36,080 --> 00:39:38,880
Back then, they needed a stone called lapis lazuli...

943
00:39:38,880 --> 00:39:41,750
to create this shade of blue.

944
00:39:42,150 --> 00:39:43,950
But you couldn't get that in Europe.

945
00:39:43,950 --> 00:39:45,990
The nearest source was Afghanistan.

946
00:39:46,220 --> 00:39:48,090
And it could only be mined briefly during summer...

947
00:39:48,090 --> 00:39:49,560
at the Sar-e-Sang mine,

948
00:39:49,560 --> 00:39:52,730
located 3,000m above sea level in the Hindu Kush Mountains.

949
00:39:53,430 --> 00:39:55,330
So it was really rare.

950
00:39:55,830 --> 00:39:57,900
To create this shade of blue,

951
00:39:57,900 --> 00:40:00,740
they traded lapis lazuli for 24-karat pure gold...

952
00:40:00,740 --> 00:40:02,440
at a 1-to-1 ratio or higher.

953
00:40:05,010 --> 00:40:07,840
So painting this with blue...

954
00:40:07,840 --> 00:40:11,110
would have cost more than painting it with pure gold.

955
00:40:11,110 --> 00:40:13,450
- Really?
- It costs more than pure gold.

956
00:40:13,450 --> 00:40:15,250
- It costs more than pure gold.
- That's right.

957
00:40:15,250 --> 00:40:16,920
Then, you'd wonder...

958
00:40:16,920 --> 00:40:18,850
who created this...

959
00:40:18,850 --> 00:40:20,220
when it'd have cost a huge fortune.

960
00:40:20,220 --> 00:40:22,460
Right. Why did the person choose to do this?

961
00:40:22,460 --> 00:40:23,860
(Who built the Scrovegni Chapel?)

962
00:40:25,030 --> 00:40:27,460
It was built by a man called Enrico Scrovegni.

963
00:40:27,600 --> 00:40:29,800
He was one of the leading bankers...

964
00:40:29,800 --> 00:40:32,100
in Padua, northern Italy.

965
00:40:32,100 --> 00:40:35,810
But he didn't earn money in such a good way.

966
00:40:35,810 --> 00:40:38,070
His father was a ruthless moneylender.

967
00:40:38,070 --> 00:40:39,540
(Enrico Scrovegni's father was a ruthless moneylender.)

968
00:40:39,540 --> 00:40:41,980
Back then, moneylending at excessive interest...

969
00:40:41,980 --> 00:40:45,110
was one of the worst sins in the Christian society.

970
00:40:46,250 --> 00:40:49,080
Since his father sinned a lot,

971
00:40:49,080 --> 00:40:50,420
where would he go when he dies?

972
00:40:50,420 --> 00:40:51,890
- The inferno.
- The inferno.

973
00:40:51,890 --> 00:40:53,720
He built this because he didn't want to go there.

974
00:40:54,220 --> 00:40:55,720
- So he wouldn't go there.
- Yes.

975
00:40:55,860 --> 00:40:57,290
If you look over there,

976
00:40:57,290 --> 00:40:59,090
he's offering the chapel.

977
00:40:59,660 --> 00:41:00,830
Really?

978
00:41:00,830 --> 00:41:03,730
(Please send me to paradise.)

979
00:41:03,930 --> 00:41:06,540
It would have been really hard to recreate this as well.

980
00:41:06,540 --> 00:41:09,210
They didn't just recreate the painting.

981
00:41:09,210 --> 00:41:11,810
They perfectly recreated the form of the ceiling,

982
00:41:11,810 --> 00:41:14,140
the curvature of the wall,

983
00:41:14,140 --> 00:41:16,280
and even the texture.

984
00:41:16,280 --> 00:41:17,350
- Really, - They even...

985
00:41:17,350 --> 00:41:19,750
recreated the cracks on the original work.

986
00:41:20,080 --> 00:41:22,150
That's amazing.

987
00:41:22,450 --> 00:41:25,220
This was recreated over 21 years.

988
00:41:25,220 --> 00:41:26,360
(Even the marks of time have been faithfully recreated.)

989
00:41:26,690 --> 00:41:30,660
(It's like entering the actual Scrovegni Chapel.)

990
00:41:30,760 --> 00:41:32,060
I got chills.

991
00:41:32,760 --> 00:41:33,800
The air conditioning is a bit strong, right?

992
00:41:33,800 --> 00:41:36,530
This is why I recommend seeing this place.

993
00:41:36,530 --> 00:41:38,770
When you go to the actual place,

994
00:41:38,770 --> 00:41:41,570
only 25 people are allowed in at a time,

995
00:41:41,570 --> 00:41:43,410
and they're given 15 minutes to look around.

996
00:41:43,410 --> 00:41:44,410
To prevent damage.

997
00:41:44,410 --> 00:41:45,770
You're asked to leave after 15 minutes.

998
00:41:45,980 --> 00:41:47,580
- It doesn't matter here.
- It doesn't matter here.

999
00:41:47,580 --> 00:41:48,610
This place...

1000
00:41:48,610 --> 00:41:50,580
- That's what happens.
- Right.

1001
00:41:50,980 --> 00:41:52,450
- We can sit here, right?
- We can.

1002
00:41:52,450 --> 00:41:54,150
- Of course.
- We can sit.

1003
00:41:54,150 --> 00:41:55,550
Goodness.

1004
00:41:58,090 --> 00:41:59,150
It's so nice.

1005
00:42:04,160 --> 00:42:07,960
(The artwork slowly leaves its mark on the eyes and the heart.)

1006
00:42:09,600 --> 00:42:11,270
I honestly recommend coming to this place.

1007
00:42:11,270 --> 00:42:12,370
- Okay.
- I see why.

1008
00:42:12,370 --> 00:42:14,170
You must see that when you visit the Otsuka Museum of Art.

1009
00:42:14,170 --> 00:42:15,770
- The Scrovegni Chapel.
- You have to know it to see it.

1010
00:42:16,210 --> 00:42:17,210
Goodness.

1011
00:42:17,210 --> 00:42:18,310
You have to know it to see it.

1012
00:42:19,110 --> 00:42:21,640
How big is this place?

1013
00:42:21,740 --> 00:42:23,080
(The youngest is full of questions.)

1014
00:42:23,080 --> 00:42:25,150
I'm not sure how big it is.

1015
00:42:25,150 --> 00:42:26,950
The route is about four kilometres long.

1016
00:42:26,950 --> 00:42:29,420
You can't... This is a shame.

1017
00:42:29,690 --> 00:42:32,050
They reproduced every detail exactly as it was carved.

1018
00:42:32,050 --> 00:42:33,120
("Empress Theodora and Her Attendants")

1019
00:42:33,120 --> 00:42:34,260
(A Byzantine mosaic on the altar wall, completed around 547)

1020
00:42:34,260 --> 00:42:37,660
(Nam Gil is captivated by the mosaic.)

1021
00:42:37,660 --> 00:42:38,790
Nam Gil.

1022
00:42:38,790 --> 00:42:40,730
- I saw the original work.
- You did?

1023
00:42:40,730 --> 00:42:42,030
- It looks exactly the same.
- The same?

1024
00:42:42,030 --> 00:42:43,400
It looks the same, but you couldn't touch it.

1025
00:42:43,400 --> 00:42:46,270
You had to line up to see it.

1026
00:42:46,270 --> 00:42:47,900
I stood in the line for 30 minutes,

1027
00:42:47,900 --> 00:42:49,810
looked at it, and went away.

1028
00:42:50,140 --> 00:42:51,610
But here, you can touch this.

1029
00:42:51,610 --> 00:42:52,710
It looks just the same.

1030
00:42:53,210 --> 00:42:55,040
- It looks exactly the same.
- It feels weird.

1031
00:42:55,610 --> 00:42:57,580
Also, there are a lot more people there.

1032
00:43:01,520 --> 00:43:03,090
There are more things to see upstairs.

1033
00:43:03,450 --> 00:43:05,020
They really did a good job here.

1034
00:43:05,150 --> 00:43:07,220
This is the third basement level.

1035
00:43:07,220 --> 00:43:08,620
It goes up to the second floor.

1036
00:43:08,620 --> 00:43:10,860
It's almost been 30 years since this place was built.

1037
00:43:10,860 --> 00:43:12,390
This is really amazing.

1038
00:43:13,400 --> 00:43:15,500
All the artworks here would be familiar to you.

1039
00:43:15,500 --> 00:43:17,770
- You know them.
- You've seen them somewhere.

1040
00:43:18,670 --> 00:43:20,800
There are so many of them.

1041
00:43:20,800 --> 00:43:21,970
It's amazing.

1042
00:43:21,970 --> 00:43:23,910
This one is really famous too.

1043
00:43:25,170 --> 00:43:26,480
I've seen them before.

1044
00:43:26,480 --> 00:43:27,480
("Young Flautist," French painter Édouard Manet's 1866 masterpiece)

1045
00:43:27,980 --> 00:43:30,180
What was that one?

1046
00:43:30,180 --> 00:43:31,180
Which one?

1047
00:43:31,180 --> 00:43:32,850
That one is famous too.

1048
00:43:32,850 --> 00:43:35,050
(A work by French painter Jean-François Millet)

1049
00:43:35,280 --> 00:43:37,250
- You can think of it...
- We've seen them.

1050
00:43:37,250 --> 00:43:38,620
as having pretty much every famous work...

1051
00:43:38,620 --> 00:43:40,060
you've seen in an art book.

1052
00:43:40,320 --> 00:43:43,260
Every piece is a masterpiece.

1053
00:43:43,260 --> 00:43:44,430
That's right.

1054
00:43:45,290 --> 00:43:46,560
Once you start looking, there's no end to it.

1055
00:43:46,560 --> 00:43:48,760
There are many famous works that anyone would recognise.

1056
00:43:48,760 --> 00:43:49,830
(How Nam Gil appreciates art: 1. Getting angry)

1057
00:43:50,030 --> 00:43:51,430
What is it? Do you want to decorate your room like this?

1058
00:43:51,430 --> 00:43:52,740
No.

1059
00:43:52,740 --> 00:43:54,740
There are works by Caravaggio too.

1060
00:43:54,740 --> 00:43:56,870
(2. Taking pictures)

1061
00:43:57,610 --> 00:43:59,210
You've gone off route.

1062
00:43:59,210 --> 00:44:00,340
(3. Going off route to appreciate artworks)

1063
00:44:00,340 --> 00:44:01,410
Goodness.

1064
00:44:05,910 --> 00:44:08,780
(Wait for me.)

1065
00:44:09,520 --> 00:44:10,950
I'll just keep going without explaining.

1066
00:44:11,290 --> 00:44:12,990
Let's come again after he's gone.

1067
00:44:14,720 --> 00:44:16,230
(The 3 brothers have been touring for 1 hour already.)

1068
00:44:16,230 --> 00:44:18,360
- Why is time going so fast?
- We should have eaten quicker.

1069
00:44:19,460 --> 00:44:21,830
Isn't it amazing how they recreated all these?

1070
00:44:21,830 --> 00:44:22,830
Right.

1071
00:44:23,430 --> 00:44:24,970
- Shall we take a look at this too?
- This is crazy.

1072
00:44:25,400 --> 00:44:27,670
- Shall we take a look at this too?
- This is crazy.

1073
00:44:31,540 --> 00:44:32,540
This...

1074
00:44:34,340 --> 00:44:35,910
- This is 10m long.
- This is no joke.

1075
00:44:36,780 --> 00:44:39,580
(The grandeur seeps through the curtains.)

1076
00:44:40,380 --> 00:44:42,280
You can find this in the Louvre.

1077
00:44:42,280 --> 00:44:44,290
It's called "The Coronation of Napoleon."

1078
00:44:44,950 --> 00:44:48,790
(Work you can see in Paris, now in Tokushima too)

1079
00:44:49,160 --> 00:44:52,190
When Napoleon first saw this painting,

1080
00:44:52,190 --> 00:44:54,400
he was in awe.

1081
00:44:55,660 --> 00:44:57,500
"This isn't a painting."

1082
00:44:59,000 --> 00:45:02,810
"It feels like I could enter this painting right now."

1083
00:45:04,010 --> 00:45:07,380
"I pay my respects to David." That's what he said.

1084
00:45:24,790 --> 00:45:26,100
Goodness.

1085
00:45:26,960 --> 00:45:31,200
Here's why Napoleon admired it so much.

1086
00:45:31,470 --> 00:45:34,600
There are approximately 200 people in the painting.

1087
00:45:34,740 --> 00:45:36,070
And he didn't just paint them.

1088
00:45:36,540 --> 00:45:39,370
He asked them to come and model for him...

1089
00:45:39,610 --> 00:45:42,180
and draw the portrait of every single person.

1090
00:45:42,380 --> 00:45:44,080
(Amazed)

1091
00:45:44,680 --> 00:45:47,020
It took him three years to finish it.

1092
00:45:52,150 --> 00:45:53,360
That's incredible.

1093
00:45:53,360 --> 00:45:54,760
(He even captured the people's minds through facial expressions.)

1094
00:45:55,260 --> 00:45:57,630
Their facial expressions and eyes are...

1095
00:45:57,630 --> 00:45:59,430
- They're realistic.
- all realistic.

1096
00:46:01,430 --> 00:46:02,500
That's something.

1097
00:46:05,730 --> 00:46:07,900
There's one distinct feature too.

1098
00:46:07,900 --> 00:46:10,140
The painting is titled "The Coronation of Napoleon."

1099
00:46:10,310 --> 00:46:12,240
But what's Napoleon doing in it?

1100
00:46:16,480 --> 00:46:17,580
- He's presenting the crown.
- He's presenting it.

1101
00:46:17,580 --> 00:46:18,680
He's presenting the crown.

1102
00:46:19,950 --> 00:46:21,080
To whom?

1103
00:46:21,780 --> 00:46:24,120
- The Empress.
- Empress Josephine in front of him.

1104
00:46:24,450 --> 00:46:26,790
This is actually the coronation of the empress.

1105
00:46:27,090 --> 00:46:28,820
I'll tell you why.

1106
00:46:29,060 --> 00:46:31,390
The only person that this man failed to conquer...

1107
00:46:31,390 --> 00:46:32,830
was Josephine kneeling before him.

1108
00:46:32,830 --> 00:46:35,200
(The only woman Emperor Napoleon couldn't conquer)

1109
00:46:35,330 --> 00:46:38,500
She was the ultimate courtesan of France.

1110
00:46:38,730 --> 00:46:41,600
She already had two children before she met Napoleon.

1111
00:46:41,600 --> 00:46:42,600
(Exclaiming)

1112
00:46:42,770 --> 00:46:45,740
Yet, Napoleon was so deeply in love and held on to her.

1113
00:46:45,940 --> 00:46:47,410
Yes, I heard about that.

1114
00:46:47,410 --> 00:46:50,050
Josephine had never submitted...

1115
00:46:50,050 --> 00:46:51,550
to Napoleon before this.

1116
00:46:51,550 --> 00:46:54,220
But to become the empress,

1117
00:46:54,520 --> 00:46:57,050
- she must kneel before the emperor.
- Right.

1118
00:46:57,050 --> 00:46:58,850
And he painted that scene.

1119
00:46:59,690 --> 00:47:01,860
- It's recorded.
- Exactly. It's been recorded.

1120
00:47:02,560 --> 00:47:05,660
"Josephine will kneel before me forever," said Napoleon.

1121
00:47:05,860 --> 00:47:08,130
At least, there's a rumour about that.

1122
00:47:08,130 --> 00:47:09,970
(Napoleon's long-cherished wish came true in the painting.)

1123
00:47:12,530 --> 00:47:15,700
As you can see, the painting is over 10m.

1124
00:47:15,870 --> 00:47:17,710
It's so big...

1125
00:47:17,870 --> 00:47:19,810
- that it's wider than the gate.
- You're right.

1126
00:47:19,810 --> 00:47:22,010
They thought about what to do with it...

1127
00:47:22,140 --> 00:47:23,850
and ended up bringing it in...

1128
00:47:23,850 --> 00:47:26,150
and hung it before they built the walls.

1129
00:47:26,150 --> 00:47:28,250
- Really?
- Then, they built this wall.

1130
00:47:28,380 --> 00:47:30,550
So, they can't get it out, even if they want to.

1131
00:47:30,950 --> 00:47:31,990
They can move it.

1132
00:47:31,990 --> 00:47:34,160
They probably won't.

1133
00:47:34,460 --> 00:47:36,330
They'll need an excavator to destroy the building.

1134
00:47:38,690 --> 00:47:39,860
If I could,

1135
00:47:39,860 --> 00:47:42,230
I would walk you through all the paintings here,

1136
00:47:42,230 --> 00:47:43,730
but we'll only check the important ones...

1137
00:47:43,730 --> 00:47:45,100
- due to time constraints.
- Okay.

1138
00:47:45,200 --> 00:47:48,070
This is the painting that you must never miss here.

1139
00:47:50,440 --> 00:47:51,710
It's that thing.

1140
00:47:51,970 --> 00:47:53,610
- What is it?
- "The Last Supper."

1141
00:47:53,610 --> 00:47:54,910
- That's right.
- Right?

1142
00:47:55,640 --> 00:47:56,880
That's insane.

1143
00:47:57,710 --> 00:47:59,080
They perfectly reproduced the damaged area.

1144
00:47:59,080 --> 00:48:00,480
- We can even touch it.
- Goodness.

1145
00:48:00,750 --> 00:48:01,820
Oh, goodness.

1146
00:48:03,250 --> 00:48:05,050
(Laughing)

1147
00:48:05,390 --> 00:48:08,720
It took about 21 years to restore it.

1148
00:48:09,290 --> 00:48:10,930
And that's the one...

1149
00:48:10,930 --> 00:48:14,360
we've been seeing since 1999 after it was restored.

1150
00:48:14,830 --> 00:48:16,670
- Oh, that's the restored version?
- Yes.

1151
00:48:16,770 --> 00:48:18,100
This is after the restoration.

1152
00:48:18,100 --> 00:48:19,300
(After restoration)

1153
00:48:20,000 --> 00:48:21,400
This is before the restoration.

1154
00:48:21,400 --> 00:48:23,910
(Before restoration)

1155
00:48:23,970 --> 00:48:25,970
If you see the painting in Milan,

1156
00:48:25,970 --> 00:48:27,210
this is the only version you get.

1157
00:48:28,410 --> 00:48:30,550
But you can see how it was before they restored it.

1158
00:48:30,550 --> 00:48:31,780
We can see that here.

1159
00:48:31,780 --> 00:48:33,950
We can't see the previous version in Milan.

1160
00:48:33,950 --> 00:48:35,080
Because it's already been restored.

1161
00:48:35,250 --> 00:48:36,550
(You can see the painting before and after restoration.)

1162
00:48:36,550 --> 00:48:37,750
That's incredible.

1163
00:48:39,250 --> 00:48:41,190
There's so much explaining to do here.

1164
00:48:41,190 --> 00:48:43,260
I could talk about this piece for an hour.

1165
00:48:43,260 --> 00:48:45,360
- Exactly.
- It's really...

1166
00:48:50,470 --> 00:48:51,970
Let's check out "Mona Lisa."

1167
00:48:52,030 --> 00:48:54,000
- They have "Mona Lisa" too?
- We have to see it.

1168
00:48:54,100 --> 00:48:55,140
Ta-da!

1169
00:48:55,400 --> 00:48:57,110
(An excited 50-year-old man)

1170
00:48:57,310 --> 00:48:58,370
Ta-da!

1171
00:49:02,140 --> 00:49:03,210
This is so...

1172
00:49:04,210 --> 00:49:06,450
"Mona Lisa" is currently in the Louvre.

1173
00:49:06,450 --> 00:49:08,850
You can see the pieces from all over the world here.

1174
00:49:08,980 --> 00:49:10,520
When you actually go there,

1175
00:49:10,920 --> 00:49:12,990
- people are lined up...
- It's packed, right?

1176
00:49:12,990 --> 00:49:14,520
- going like this.
- I've seen that.

1177
00:49:14,520 --> 00:49:15,760
And they're all doing this with their phones.

1178
00:49:15,760 --> 00:49:17,030
Yes, I saw that too.

1179
00:49:17,030 --> 00:49:20,030
- You can't even see it.
- It's jam-packed with people.

1180
00:49:20,030 --> 00:49:22,230
And there are always pickpockets among them.

1181
00:49:22,230 --> 00:49:23,930
(The toughest smile to see in the whole world)

1182
00:49:26,870 --> 00:49:29,570
This art museum encourages you to have fun,

1183
00:49:29,710 --> 00:49:31,610
so there are lots of fun activities.

1184
00:49:33,980 --> 00:49:35,240
(Art Cosplay Festival 2026 for limited time only)

1185
00:49:35,240 --> 00:49:36,380
(You can wear the costumes and props from the paintings.)

1186
00:49:36,380 --> 00:49:37,380
Gosh.

1187
00:49:37,380 --> 00:49:38,610
I'm so jealous. You don't even need to do anything...

1188
00:49:38,610 --> 00:49:39,820
to look like a painting.

1189
00:49:39,980 --> 00:49:41,680
- He's just standing there.
- He's gifted.

1190
00:49:41,680 --> 00:49:43,950
I know, right? He's effortlessly funny.

1191
00:49:44,090 --> 00:49:45,190
"Jun Moo Lisa."

1192
00:49:46,490 --> 00:49:48,390
(Humming)

1193
00:49:48,390 --> 00:49:49,420
All done?

1194
00:49:50,760 --> 00:49:52,660
- It suits you.
- You look pretty.

1195
00:49:52,660 --> 00:49:53,800
- You are.
- This is it, right?

1196
00:49:53,800 --> 00:49:54,860
It suits you so well.

1197
00:49:55,800 --> 00:49:57,000
- That's amazing.
- But...

1198
00:49:57,000 --> 00:49:58,130
Close your mouth.

1199
00:49:58,530 --> 00:50:01,270
- I didn't know how pretty you were.
- Look at this.

1200
00:50:01,970 --> 00:50:03,000
Hang on.

1201
00:50:03,000 --> 00:50:05,310
(You can already see what's coming this time.)

1202
00:50:05,610 --> 00:50:07,180
What am I supposed to do?

1203
00:50:08,180 --> 00:50:09,750
Why do I get the one that's...

1204
00:50:09,750 --> 00:50:11,250
- You're an actor.
- It's so unfair.

1205
00:50:11,250 --> 00:50:12,950
You're a Hollywood actor.

1206
00:50:16,180 --> 00:50:18,420
- Now, that's a Hollywood actor.
- Right?

1207
00:50:21,390 --> 00:50:22,960
You have to go inside.

1208
00:50:23,260 --> 00:50:24,860
Tell yourself that Vincent is watching.

1209
00:50:28,900 --> 00:50:31,000
- Last one?
- Let's check it out.

1210
00:50:31,400 --> 00:50:33,570
Many people overlook it, but you must see this one.

1211
00:50:35,470 --> 00:50:36,710
It's dedicated to Vincent van Gogh.

1212
00:50:37,570 --> 00:50:39,940
- Vincent van Gogh.
- Vincent van Gogh.

1213
00:50:40,310 --> 00:50:43,180
(A tragic artist beloved around the world)

1214
00:50:43,310 --> 00:50:45,410
("Starry Night Over the Rhône," capturing the night of Arles)

1215
00:50:45,410 --> 00:50:47,320
("The Bedroom in Arles," depicting his own bedroom)

1216
00:50:52,920 --> 00:50:54,190
This is so cool.

1217
00:50:55,260 --> 00:50:57,130
What's meaningful about this hall is...

1218
00:50:57,130 --> 00:51:00,060
that you normally can't see all seven sunflowers at once.

1219
00:51:01,200 --> 00:51:03,170
- They're scattered.
- They're all over the world.

1220
00:51:03,530 --> 00:51:05,370
But you can appreciate all of them here.

1221
00:51:05,670 --> 00:51:06,700
This is an all-in-one tour.

1222
00:51:06,700 --> 00:51:08,470
- It really is!
- I told you so during the opening.

1223
00:51:08,470 --> 00:51:10,070
That we'd be able to see everything in one go.

1224
00:51:10,170 --> 00:51:11,540
- It's an all-in-one tour.
- It really is.

1225
00:51:11,540 --> 00:51:12,640
I told you so.

1226
00:51:12,640 --> 00:51:13,810
(Philadelphia Museum of Art, Sompo Museum of Art,)

1227
00:51:13,810 --> 00:51:15,140
(lost during World War 2, phantom masterpiece)

1228
00:51:15,140 --> 00:51:16,510
(The pride of Otsuka Museum of Art)

1229
00:51:16,510 --> 00:51:18,280
(Van Gogh's Sunflowers Exhibition Hall)

1230
00:51:20,520 --> 00:51:21,680
Where do you think you're going?

1231
00:51:22,350 --> 00:51:23,720
I have to get going now.

1232
00:51:24,050 --> 00:51:26,420
It's not like we're in Daejeon.

1233
00:51:26,690 --> 00:51:28,760
- It's Japan.
- I know, but...

1234
00:51:29,260 --> 00:51:30,890
I took a flight this morning,

1235
00:51:30,890 --> 00:51:33,760
so I have to leave now to catch the evening flight.

1236
00:51:34,030 --> 00:51:35,360
I'm sure your wife will understand.

1237
00:51:35,930 --> 00:51:37,400
Let's pray so that he stays alive.

1238
00:51:39,700 --> 00:51:41,100
We pray that there's peace...

1239
00:51:41,100 --> 00:51:42,840
- in Chang Yong's family.
- I'll see you next time.

1240
00:51:42,840 --> 00:51:44,010
- Thanks for coming.
- Bye, guys.

1241
00:51:44,010 --> 00:51:45,610
- Thank you.
- Thank you for today!

1242
00:51:45,610 --> 00:51:46,910
Bye. See you.

1243
00:51:47,010 --> 00:51:48,840
- Don't miss the flight.
- Bye.

1244
00:51:49,410 --> 00:51:51,250
It feels completely different after his explanation.

1245
00:51:51,250 --> 00:51:52,410
- Obviously.
- I would have been clueless...

1246
00:51:52,410 --> 00:51:53,780
- if he hadn't come here.
- That's true.

1247
00:51:54,080 --> 00:51:55,350
That was so fun.

1248
00:51:57,620 --> 00:51:59,220
(The trio decides to stay a little longer.)

1249
00:51:59,220 --> 00:52:00,290
This is...

1250
00:52:00,860 --> 00:52:02,960
- There are so many.
- It's awesome.

1251
00:52:07,300 --> 00:52:10,270
Personally, the art museum will be very memorable.

1252
00:52:10,800 --> 00:52:12,770
I was fascinated as I was experiencing it.

1253
00:52:13,070 --> 00:52:15,500
- Right.
- I never knew I could.

1254
00:52:15,500 --> 00:52:17,870
If I had watched this show on TV,

1255
00:52:17,870 --> 00:52:19,570
I would have come here just for this.

1256
00:52:19,980 --> 00:52:21,210
That's how fun it was.

1257
00:52:34,860 --> 00:52:37,160
- Where do we sleep tonight, though?
- Sleep?

1258
00:52:37,390 --> 00:52:39,630
Are you tired? It's almost bedtime, right?

1259
00:52:39,860 --> 00:52:42,130
Well, it's only 6pm, but...

1260
00:52:42,300 --> 00:52:44,270
- You should sleep early.
- I was just curious.

1261
00:52:44,270 --> 00:52:46,300
You can look forward to today's stay.

1262
00:52:48,240 --> 00:52:50,570
Just for the three of us,

1263
00:52:50,940 --> 00:52:53,540
we found a reasonably priced,

1264
00:52:54,710 --> 00:52:56,340
unshared house.

1265
00:52:57,650 --> 00:53:00,580
I'm just grateful that I can sleep indoors.

1266
00:53:01,720 --> 00:53:02,980
I'm fine with everything.

1267
00:53:04,350 --> 00:53:05,790
This is so peaceful.

1268
00:53:06,150 --> 00:53:07,390
Look at the sunset and everything.

1269
00:53:09,460 --> 00:53:11,130
Japanese rural areas are fascinating.

1270
00:53:13,900 --> 00:53:15,830
The houses look beautiful.

1271
00:53:17,430 --> 00:53:18,970
Houses in Japan are so cute.

1272
00:53:18,970 --> 00:53:20,840
- Right?
- That's what I like about Japan.

1273
00:53:20,840 --> 00:53:22,140
It's classical.

1274
00:53:22,140 --> 00:53:23,740
They mostly keep it minimal.

1275
00:53:26,540 --> 00:53:30,210
(The three drive between minimal houses.)

1276
00:53:30,210 --> 00:53:31,850
- There's a house here?
- Yes.

1277
00:53:34,820 --> 00:53:36,120
- We're here.
- Thanks.

1278
00:53:36,120 --> 00:53:37,290
All right!

1279
00:53:37,290 --> 00:53:38,350
(From Otsuka Museum of Art to their stay)

1280
00:53:38,350 --> 00:53:39,960
Great work, everyone.

1281
00:53:40,120 --> 00:53:42,460
(Welcome, travellers.)

1282
00:53:43,020 --> 00:53:44,060
Great.

1283
00:53:44,060 --> 00:53:47,660
(6:30pm, accommodation)

1284
00:53:47,800 --> 00:53:49,130
Isn't it atmospheric?

1285
00:53:49,630 --> 00:53:50,870
- It's nice.
- It is.

1286
00:53:50,870 --> 00:53:52,370
I already love the front yard.

1287
00:53:52,930 --> 00:53:54,640
- I can't wait to go inside.
- This place is nice.

1288
00:53:54,900 --> 00:53:57,640
(He stops and appreciates the view.)

1289
00:53:58,970 --> 00:54:01,110
Even the houses next to us...

1290
00:54:05,280 --> 00:54:06,680
- Let's see.
- It's spacious.

1291
00:54:06,820 --> 00:54:08,050
(Exclaiming)

1292
00:54:08,050 --> 00:54:10,190
- Come in.
- Come on in.

1293
00:54:10,590 --> 00:54:11,850
Put your suitcases here.

1294
00:54:13,220 --> 00:54:14,390
You want to bring it upstairs?

1295
00:54:15,090 --> 00:54:17,660
- It's just like what I saw.
- This is lovely.

1296
00:54:17,660 --> 00:54:19,190
It looks good!

1297
00:54:20,330 --> 00:54:22,660
- It's that famous Japanese...
- Tatami.

1298
00:54:22,930 --> 00:54:24,970
- Cool.
- I love it here.

1299
00:54:25,300 --> 00:54:27,200
Yes, the table comes off, right?

1300
00:54:34,980 --> 00:54:36,440
Right, this is it.

1301
00:54:38,250 --> 00:54:39,350
This is it.

1302
00:54:40,750 --> 00:54:42,650
(I'll enjoy the food.)

1303
00:54:42,650 --> 00:54:44,790
The second room on the first floor is a tatami room.

1304
00:54:45,720 --> 00:54:47,960
Goodness, it's nice.

1305
00:54:49,260 --> 00:54:51,130
(The warm traditional light completes the mood.)

1306
00:54:51,130 --> 00:54:53,130
Just look at this mood.

1307
00:54:53,130 --> 00:54:55,200
- I haven't seen this in a while.
- Gosh.

1308
00:54:55,200 --> 00:54:57,870
I love it here.

1309
00:55:00,200 --> 00:55:01,340
Here's the bathroom.

1310
00:55:01,340 --> 00:55:02,740
It's a minimal Japanese bathroom.

1311
00:55:02,740 --> 00:55:06,310
(A bathroom showcasing Japanese minimalism)

1312
00:55:08,040 --> 00:55:10,680
Does anyone need an upstairs room? Shall we take a look?

1313
00:55:11,080 --> 00:55:12,150
Oh, gosh.

1314
00:55:12,480 --> 00:55:13,750
The house looks unreal.

1315
00:55:15,150 --> 00:55:16,620
- It looks like a set, doesn't it?
- It does.

1316
00:55:16,620 --> 00:55:17,690
Goodness.

1317
00:55:17,950 --> 00:55:19,650
(A hallway welcomes visitors with its clean, understated colours.)

1318
00:55:20,060 --> 00:55:21,220
A tatami room.

1319
00:55:23,260 --> 00:55:24,490
Oh, tatami.

1320
00:55:24,490 --> 00:55:25,960
(The second oldest seems to love the place.)

1321
00:55:26,390 --> 00:55:27,730
Tatami has its own charm too.

1322
00:55:27,860 --> 00:55:29,000
- It certainly does.
- Exactly.

1323
00:55:30,570 --> 00:55:32,170
- There are so many rooms.
- Oh, there's another one here.

1324
00:55:32,170 --> 00:55:33,330
Oh, this is nice.

1325
00:55:35,970 --> 00:55:37,170
It's spacious.

1326
00:55:37,170 --> 00:55:40,040
(Another spacious room with three beds side by side)

1327
00:55:41,640 --> 00:55:45,450
(The endless room tour: another room for 2)

1328
00:55:45,950 --> 00:55:49,720
(A whole house full of Japanese charm)

1329
00:55:49,950 --> 00:55:51,420
I'm curious how much this costs.

1330
00:55:51,420 --> 00:55:53,020
- How much would it be?
- Three hundred dollars.

1331
00:55:53,020 --> 00:55:54,220
(Entire house: 300 dollars for 1 night)

1332
00:55:54,220 --> 00:55:55,290
And it's the whole house.

1333
00:55:55,290 --> 00:55:57,090
Even for the 3 of us, it's 100, 100, 100 dollars.

1334
00:55:57,230 --> 00:55:58,960
Right. It's 100 dollars per night.

1335
00:55:58,960 --> 00:56:00,060
If ten people come...

1336
00:56:00,300 --> 00:56:02,460
- If 10 people come...
- How much for 10 people?

1337
00:56:03,130 --> 00:56:04,330
Thirty dollars.

1338
00:56:05,170 --> 00:56:06,330
Hey, just pay 30 dollars.

1339
00:56:09,770 --> 00:56:12,340
Nice. Open them all.

1340
00:56:12,570 --> 00:56:13,610
Oh, this is nice.

1341
00:56:13,610 --> 00:56:16,240
You really get a feel for rural Japan here.

1342
00:56:17,780 --> 00:56:20,480
(Like a scene from a movie)

1343
00:56:20,480 --> 00:56:23,790
(The tranquil Japanese countryside)

1344
00:56:23,790 --> 00:56:25,620
- Shall we walk around the village?
- Sure.

1345
00:56:25,620 --> 00:56:27,720
It's a pretty village, so let's have a look around.

1346
00:56:27,720 --> 00:56:30,330
It's scary outside too once it gets dark.

1347
00:56:31,890 --> 00:56:32,960
Let's go out.

1348
00:56:34,660 --> 00:56:35,900
Oh, look at that cat.

1349
00:56:36,530 --> 00:56:37,830
It's living a good life.

1350
00:56:38,430 --> 00:56:40,470
Its tongue's out because it's hot.

1351
00:56:40,670 --> 00:56:42,140
Oh, what a cute little thing.

1352
00:56:43,100 --> 00:56:44,240
Oh, look at this.

1353
00:56:44,840 --> 00:56:46,440
- The moon's out too.
- It's so beautiful.

1354
00:56:50,210 --> 00:56:51,610
It's so charming. So charming.

1355
00:56:52,150 --> 00:56:53,210
It's picturesque too.

1356
00:56:54,520 --> 00:56:55,920
It's peaceful.

1357
00:56:57,250 --> 00:57:00,420
(A sleepy village where time seems to slow down)

1358
00:57:00,620 --> 00:57:01,960
It's amazing there's a softshell turtle here.

1359
00:57:02,560 --> 00:57:03,790
There's a softshell turtle?

1360
00:57:03,790 --> 00:57:04,930
- Yes, there it is.
- Where?

1361
00:57:04,930 --> 00:57:06,490
It's crawling over there.

1362
00:57:06,730 --> 00:57:08,200
It's moving slowly.

1363
00:57:08,200 --> 00:57:10,300
Over there. Its head's above the water.

1364
00:57:10,770 --> 00:57:11,770
Oh, I see it.

1365
00:57:11,770 --> 00:57:13,330
These guys must be Japanese, right?

1366
00:57:13,800 --> 00:57:14,940
What do you mean?

1367
00:57:16,500 --> 00:57:17,840
- Are they from China?
- No, that's not what I mean.

1368
00:57:18,270 --> 00:57:19,340
They're native to Japan.

1369
00:57:19,340 --> 00:57:21,610
(We're Japanese natives.)

1370
00:57:22,780 --> 00:57:24,550
- A train's coming.
- Oh, what an atmosphere.

1371
00:57:25,650 --> 00:57:26,920
It's like a Japanese anime.

1372
00:57:28,720 --> 00:57:29,920
And there's a turtle down below.

1373
00:57:29,920 --> 00:57:32,150
(Crossing bells ring softly in a scene straight out of anime.)

1374
00:57:32,590 --> 00:57:34,520
Shall we go meet up now?

1375
00:57:37,460 --> 00:57:39,890
(Somehow, it leaves them feeling warm and fuzzy.)

1376
00:57:40,200 --> 00:57:41,760
This place is picture-perfect.

1377
00:57:44,530 --> 00:57:50,510
(In a cool summer breeze and picture-perfect scenery,)

1378
00:57:53,170 --> 00:57:56,950
(they quietly wait for the train to approach.)

1379
00:57:58,780 --> 00:57:59,880
When is it coming?

1380
00:58:00,480 --> 00:58:02,220
(The train seems to be a slow poke.)

1381
00:58:02,220 --> 00:58:03,920
Why so slow? Hurry up already.

1382
00:58:04,650 --> 00:58:05,750
Let's go.

1383
00:58:06,090 --> 00:58:07,460
- Let's go.
- Tell it to forget it.

1384
00:58:08,420 --> 00:58:09,890
Maybe no train's coming. It must be a false alarm.

1385
00:58:10,120 --> 00:58:11,160
It's a false alarm.

1386
00:58:11,690 --> 00:58:13,860
I'm hungry. Let's go eat already.

1387
00:58:14,330 --> 00:58:15,560
What did you say was famous here?

1388
00:58:16,500 --> 00:58:19,300
There's a famous Japanese wagyu called Awa beef.

1389
00:58:19,470 --> 00:58:20,670
What's Awa beef?

1390
00:58:20,800 --> 00:58:22,100
What is Awa beef?

1391
00:58:22,300 --> 00:58:23,810
We're in Tokushima Prefecture.

1392
00:58:24,310 --> 00:58:27,110
Tokushima Prefecture used to be called Awa Prefecture.

1393
00:58:27,240 --> 00:58:30,080
It's beef from the Awa region.

1394
00:58:30,080 --> 00:58:31,210
Awa beef.

1395
00:58:31,350 --> 00:58:32,610
It's like Hoengseong Korean beef.

1396
00:58:34,420 --> 00:58:35,620
(A protective hand)

1397
00:58:35,980 --> 00:58:37,150
So many dump trucks pass through.

1398
00:58:37,250 --> 00:58:38,690
You're sweet.

1399
00:58:40,150 --> 00:58:41,660
(Let's go have a delicious dinner.)

1400
00:58:41,660 --> 00:58:42,790
All right, let's get in the car.

1401
00:58:43,020 --> 00:58:44,130
Hot, isn't it?

1402
00:58:46,830 --> 00:58:48,930
How is it so quiet here?

1403
00:58:49,700 --> 00:58:51,000
The Korean countryside's like this too.

1404
00:58:51,930 --> 00:58:54,800
The barbecue place we're going to is closer to downtown.

1405
00:58:55,370 --> 00:58:56,540
We have 27 minutes to go.

1406
00:58:56,910 --> 00:58:58,540
- It's quite a drive.
- It is.

1407
00:58:58,940 --> 00:59:01,080
The place we're going to stays open all night.

1408
00:59:02,380 --> 00:59:04,680
This neighbourhood really calms you.

1409
00:59:04,680 --> 00:59:06,610
Because it isn't so hectic.

1410
00:59:08,420 --> 00:59:09,550
It's more urban here, right?

1411
00:59:09,850 --> 00:59:12,850
Oh, now we're seeing neon signs.

1412
00:59:13,150 --> 00:59:16,220
There's a technical term. Neon signs.

1413
00:59:16,220 --> 00:59:17,290
Neon signs.

1414
00:59:23,670 --> 00:59:26,070
All right, we're almost there.

1415
00:59:26,800 --> 00:59:28,940
This is definitely downtown.

1416
00:59:29,970 --> 00:59:31,270
(Into the dazzling downtown...)

1417
00:59:31,270 --> 00:59:33,710
(of dazzling Tokushima)

1418
00:59:33,940 --> 00:59:37,210
(Lodging to Sakaemachi Street)

1419
00:59:37,210 --> 00:59:40,780
This is Sakaemachi, the heart of Tokushima's nightlife.

1420
00:59:41,280 --> 00:59:43,620
- It's more urban than I expected.
- Right?

1421
00:59:43,750 --> 00:59:45,750
Though not as much as Tokyo.

1422
00:59:46,120 --> 00:59:48,690
- There's not a single Korean here.
- No.

1423
00:59:48,690 --> 00:59:49,820
- Not one.
- I think that's why I feel at ease.

1424
00:59:50,060 --> 00:59:51,160
Yes, it's relaxing.

1425
00:59:54,330 --> 00:59:56,300
If this were Tokyo,

1426
00:59:56,300 --> 00:59:58,130
we'd hear people exclaim about every five minutes.

1427
00:59:58,530 --> 00:59:59,700
Exactly.

1428
01:00:00,130 --> 01:00:01,300
I'd rather be here.

1429
01:00:01,300 --> 01:00:03,100
Because if no one in Tokyo even exclaimed...

1430
01:00:03,100 --> 01:00:04,110
(It would hurt their feelings.)

1431
01:00:04,110 --> 01:00:05,170
That would be the worst.

1432
01:00:05,170 --> 01:00:06,270
(It would hurt their feelings.)

1433
01:00:06,410 --> 01:00:07,440
We need to work hard.

1434
01:00:07,440 --> 01:00:08,980
(We need to work harder.)

1435
01:00:10,080 --> 01:00:11,810
- It looks great.
- What an atmosphere.

1436
01:00:20,390 --> 01:00:21,520
This way, please.

1437
01:00:22,290 --> 01:00:25,760
Head in here, under the sign that says Sakaemachi.

1438
01:00:26,630 --> 01:00:27,730
Sakaemachi.

1439
01:00:33,570 --> 01:00:36,970
(Sakaemachi Street)

1440
01:00:37,170 --> 01:00:38,640
Oh, I'm really hungry.

1441
01:00:39,170 --> 01:00:41,210
- It's in here?
- Yes.

1442
01:00:41,640 --> 01:00:43,140
- Come this way.
- All right.

1443
01:00:45,350 --> 01:00:46,450
Here it is.

1444
01:00:46,880 --> 01:00:48,450
Gyuzen.

1445
01:00:49,880 --> 01:00:51,090
This place has character.

1446
01:00:51,650 --> 01:00:53,050
It's a yakiniku restaurant.

1447
01:00:53,520 --> 01:00:55,490
Oh, I love the atmosphere.

1448
01:00:55,720 --> 01:00:56,960
It looks really old.

1449
01:00:57,730 --> 01:00:58,930
I'm looking forward to this.

1450
01:00:59,460 --> 01:01:01,360
Nice. These places have character.

1451
01:01:01,360 --> 01:01:02,430
Welcome.

1452
01:01:02,630 --> 01:01:03,800
Three, please.

1453
01:01:03,800 --> 01:01:04,800
- A party of three?
- Yes.

1454
01:01:04,800 --> 01:01:06,270
Come on in.

1455
01:01:06,900 --> 01:01:08,940
(Oh, gosh!)

1456
01:01:09,400 --> 01:01:11,440
Oh, this place has character.

1457
01:01:12,170 --> 01:01:14,140
There are lots of stairs like this in Japan, right?

1458
01:01:14,140 --> 01:01:15,910
Yes, they make good use of small spaces.

1459
01:01:15,910 --> 01:01:17,050
Right.

1460
01:01:17,780 --> 01:01:19,850
Thank you.

1461
01:01:22,450 --> 01:01:23,580
Oh, gosh.

1462
01:01:24,390 --> 01:01:25,650
- Oh, gosh.
- This is really nice.

1463
01:01:25,890 --> 01:01:28,120
- Thank you.
- I'm older, so let me sit here.

1464
01:01:28,120 --> 01:01:29,890
I need something to lean against.

1465
01:01:29,890 --> 01:01:31,030
Where should I sit?

1466
01:01:31,190 --> 01:01:33,530
Three draft beers to start, please.

1467
01:01:33,530 --> 01:01:35,500
- Certainly. One moment, please.
- Thank you.

1468
01:01:37,170 --> 01:01:39,030
We should have rice here. Ochazuke.

1469
01:01:39,400 --> 01:01:41,440
- Rice?
- Used the translator again already?

1470
01:01:41,640 --> 01:01:42,870
- Yes.
- Good job.

1471
01:01:42,970 --> 01:01:44,070
You know, at a place like this...

1472
01:01:44,070 --> 01:01:46,570
- Potato salad would be great first.
- Potato salad.

1473
01:01:46,570 --> 01:01:48,780
(The second oldest has a plan.)

1474
01:01:51,880 --> 01:01:53,310
It says Japanese Black wagyu.

1475
01:01:53,750 --> 01:01:55,480
It means black-haired.

1476
01:01:56,150 --> 01:01:57,290
Black cattle.

1477
01:01:58,120 --> 01:01:59,690
- They have kimchi.
- They do.

1478
01:01:59,690 --> 01:02:01,960
Let's order kimchi.

1479
01:02:01,960 --> 01:02:03,020
(Yakiniku restaurants often serve kimchi.)

1480
01:02:03,260 --> 01:02:05,390
Thank you for waiting.

1481
01:02:05,960 --> 01:02:07,730
Thank you.

1482
01:02:09,430 --> 01:02:12,870
(Joon, a well-known beer lover,)

1483
01:02:13,130 --> 01:02:17,370
(and former beer ad model Nam Gil start by downing cold draft beer.)

1484
01:02:17,610 --> 01:02:18,740
Is it good?

1485
01:02:20,640 --> 01:02:22,710
What dishes do you recommend?

1486
01:02:22,810 --> 01:02:23,980
Awa beef loin,

1487
01:02:24,280 --> 01:02:27,950
steak and premium short ribs are our recommendations.

1488
01:02:27,950 --> 01:02:31,820
We use A5-grade Awa beef.

1489
01:02:32,590 --> 01:02:35,720
Then we'll have one of each.

1490
01:02:35,960 --> 01:02:38,160
(Order: 1. Awa beef loin, 2. Awa beef steak)

1491
01:02:38,160 --> 01:02:41,730
(3. Premium Awa beef short ribs, 4. Kimchi)

1492
01:02:42,900 --> 01:02:46,170
(Tucked deep inside a Sakaemachi alley)

1493
01:02:46,270 --> 01:02:50,910
(A local favourite serving only A5-grade Awa beef)

1494
01:02:51,170 --> 01:02:53,210
(Serving only top-quality wagyu for 35 years)

1495
01:02:53,740 --> 01:02:57,150
(A Sakaemachi favourite famed by word of mouth)

1496
01:03:01,050 --> 01:03:02,750
Here's today's starter.

1497
01:03:02,750 --> 01:03:05,520
It's made with Awa beef round.

1498
01:03:05,520 --> 01:03:06,950
It's tataki.

1499
01:03:07,320 --> 01:03:08,860
Here's your tataki salad.

1500
01:03:09,790 --> 01:03:11,060
Tataki salad.

1501
01:03:11,060 --> 01:03:13,490
(Awa beef tataki salad)

1502
01:03:14,860 --> 01:03:16,000
Thank you for the food.

1503
01:03:16,000 --> 01:03:17,930
(Otoshi: izakayas' automatic starter that doubles as a cover charge)

1504
01:03:26,240 --> 01:03:27,340
Oh, seriously.

1505
01:03:27,780 --> 01:03:28,910
It's like going to Grandpa's...

1506
01:03:29,110 --> 01:03:30,980
- and having him casually...
- Just casually...

1507
01:03:31,380 --> 01:03:34,550
(Grandpa's rustic salad, made with care)

1508
01:03:38,650 --> 01:03:41,390
(The second oldest must have been starving.)

1509
01:03:42,460 --> 01:03:46,330
(An empty plate in just three bites)

1510
01:03:49,100 --> 01:03:50,800
You must have been really hungry.

1511
01:03:50,800 --> 01:03:52,430
I'm starving.

1512
01:03:52,430 --> 01:03:53,470
Really?

1513
01:03:55,400 --> 01:03:57,140
Here's your kimchi first.

1514
01:03:57,140 --> 01:04:00,370
- We serve kimchi from Korea.
- Thank you.

1515
01:04:00,680 --> 01:04:04,110
Here's your Awa beef loin.

1516
01:04:10,920 --> 01:04:14,090
(Awa beef loin)

1517
01:04:16,390 --> 01:04:18,090
Oh, gosh.

1518
01:04:18,090 --> 01:04:19,360
Look at that marbling.

1519
01:04:26,570 --> 01:04:29,940
(That looks delicious.)

1520
01:04:31,110 --> 01:04:33,910
It's already cooked.

1521
01:04:34,740 --> 01:04:36,310
Already? That was quick.

1522
01:04:36,510 --> 01:04:37,610
The grill's really hot.

1523
01:04:39,310 --> 01:04:43,180
(Before the meat loses its juices)

1524
01:04:43,520 --> 01:04:45,320
- Here, this...
- Go ahead.

1525
01:04:45,320 --> 01:04:46,390
- Go ahead.
- You...

1526
01:04:46,390 --> 01:04:47,690
- You first.
- Go ahead.

1527
01:04:47,690 --> 01:04:49,120
- Let's try it.
- Joon, have some.

1528
01:04:51,960 --> 01:04:53,590
It's tender. No scissors needed.

1529
01:04:53,590 --> 01:04:54,600
Really?

1530
01:04:54,600 --> 01:04:55,800
You can bite right through it.

1531
01:04:56,260 --> 01:04:57,270
Gosh!

1532
01:04:57,270 --> 01:04:58,770
Is it good?

1533
01:04:59,030 --> 01:05:00,270
I'm curious.

1534
01:05:00,900 --> 01:05:02,140
It's incredibly tender.

1535
01:05:04,540 --> 01:05:06,710
(Special sauce)

1536
01:05:17,290 --> 01:05:19,490
(The second oldest is amazed too.)

1537
01:05:19,990 --> 01:05:21,490
I'm curious!

1538
01:05:25,790 --> 01:05:26,960
Easy to bite through, right?

1539
01:05:27,500 --> 01:05:29,460
(He now 100 percent understands how the older two feel.)

1540
01:05:32,000 --> 01:05:33,800
- This is really good.
- It is, isn't it?

1541
01:05:43,840 --> 01:05:45,580
The first bite surprised me.

1542
01:05:45,580 --> 01:05:48,250
(The first bite surprised me.)

1543
01:05:48,780 --> 01:05:51,820
Yakiniku isn't usually made with top-quality meat.

1544
01:05:51,950 --> 01:05:54,660
Marinade masks the meat's flavour.

1545
01:05:55,020 --> 01:05:57,530
But this marinade isn't too strong.

1546
01:05:57,530 --> 01:05:58,830
Right?

1547
01:05:59,130 --> 01:06:00,460
That's what I like about it.

1548
01:06:02,800 --> 01:06:04,170
It's so good.

1549
01:06:04,870 --> 01:06:09,070
Yakiniku was influenced by Korean barbecue...

1550
01:06:09,070 --> 01:06:11,640
and developed into a Japanese dining tradition.

1551
01:06:11,640 --> 01:06:16,240
This laid the foundation for today's Japanese yakiniku.

1552
01:06:16,580 --> 01:06:19,380
The grilling methods differ too.

1553
01:06:19,380 --> 01:06:21,680
Koreans use large cuts of meat...

1554
01:06:21,680 --> 01:06:24,520
and cut them with scissors...

1555
01:06:24,520 --> 01:06:27,220
while grilling them to keep them moist and juicy.

1556
01:06:27,620 --> 01:06:30,730
In Japan, scissors aren't used,

1557
01:06:30,730 --> 01:06:34,430
and meat comes from the kitchen in bite-sized pieces.

1558
01:06:36,860 --> 01:06:40,370
Marinating and ageing methods also differ.

1559
01:06:40,370 --> 01:06:44,670
Koreans use a soy-based marinade for several hours to a few days...

1560
01:06:44,670 --> 01:06:47,510
to let the flavours soak in.

1561
01:06:47,510 --> 01:06:51,280
In Japan, on the other hand,

1562
01:06:51,280 --> 01:06:53,610
the meat is dipped in sauce.

1563
01:06:54,180 --> 01:06:56,680
Doesn't yakiniku look delicious?

1564
01:07:09,830 --> 01:07:11,430
(The second oldest is amazed too.)

1565
01:07:11,430 --> 01:07:13,270
Oh, gosh.

1566
01:07:13,400 --> 01:07:14,600
So good.

1567
01:07:15,600 --> 01:07:17,070
It's good because the marinade isn't overpowering.

1568
01:07:17,170 --> 01:07:18,440
(Bliss)

1569
01:07:18,440 --> 01:07:19,740
(Pure bliss)

1570
01:07:19,740 --> 01:07:21,710
Loin can get a bit too rich.

1571
01:07:22,780 --> 01:07:24,580
You really need kimchi here.

1572
01:07:31,790 --> 01:07:33,720
It cuts right through the richness.

1573
01:07:36,520 --> 01:07:40,130
Here are your premium Awa beef short ribs.

1574
01:07:40,130 --> 01:07:41,530
- Yes, short ribs.
- Here you are.

1575
01:07:41,530 --> 01:07:43,130
Thank you.

1576
01:07:47,200 --> 01:07:48,900
Oh, gosh.

1577
01:07:54,740 --> 01:07:56,910
(Premium Awa beef short ribs)

1578
01:07:57,340 --> 01:07:59,850
(Premium short ribs drizzled with sauce)

1579
01:08:03,220 --> 01:08:04,650
I think you can eat it now.

1580
01:08:09,160 --> 01:08:10,790
It's really good.

1581
01:08:16,560 --> 01:08:19,500
(Saying it's delicious gets old. Now it's almost annoying.)

1582
01:08:24,070 --> 01:08:25,170
Oh.

1583
01:08:25,470 --> 01:08:26,570
Oh.

1584
01:08:27,980 --> 01:08:29,010
Yum.

1585
01:08:29,010 --> 01:08:31,280
It's delicious from the first bite.

1586
01:08:31,810 --> 01:08:34,150
- It's good.
- Have kimchi to cut the richness.

1587
01:08:34,150 --> 01:08:35,480
This is driving me crazy.

1588
01:08:35,750 --> 01:08:37,050
It's really tender, right?

1589
01:08:37,050 --> 01:08:39,420
I'm in bliss.

1590
01:08:39,850 --> 01:08:41,120
I couldn't tell just by looking,

1591
01:08:41,120 --> 01:08:43,460
but the moment I tasted it, I went, "gosh."

1592
01:08:44,590 --> 01:08:49,030
(As they lightly dip each piece in sauce and eat it,)

1593
01:08:51,670 --> 01:08:56,770
(their hands and mouths never get a break.)

1594
01:09:06,610 --> 01:09:08,680
Oh no, it's all gone already.

1595
01:09:10,380 --> 01:09:13,120
(The next dish to ease their regret)

1596
01:09:14,320 --> 01:09:17,160
(Awa beef steak)

1597
01:09:17,420 --> 01:09:19,790
Thank you for waiting. Here's your steak.

1598
01:09:20,390 --> 01:09:21,960
(The second oldest's eyes light up at the new dish.)

1599
01:09:21,960 --> 01:09:23,600
It's steak.

1600
01:09:30,140 --> 01:09:31,870
Hyun Moo, could you order me a bowl of rice?

1601
01:09:31,870 --> 01:09:35,110
- Rice? I'd like 2 bowls, please.
- All right, 2 bowls.

1602
01:09:35,510 --> 01:09:37,650
- You really have an appetite.
- How can you eat so much?

1603
01:09:37,650 --> 01:09:39,980
Has he been going hungry?

1604
01:09:39,980 --> 01:09:41,720
(The second oldest goes all out on the meat.)

1605
01:09:42,220 --> 01:09:43,520
Oh, come on.

1606
01:09:47,690 --> 01:09:48,790
Oh, gosh.

1607
01:09:51,130 --> 01:09:53,330
Why is all the meat so good?

1608
01:09:54,300 --> 01:09:55,430
Oh, my.

1609
01:09:55,830 --> 01:09:56,930
Oh, gosh.

1610
01:09:56,930 --> 01:10:00,230
So Awa beef's defining trait is how tender it is.

1611
01:10:00,230 --> 01:10:02,000
Why is it so tender?

1612
01:10:02,270 --> 01:10:04,770
Which did you prefer? The loin or what we're eating now?

1613
01:10:05,210 --> 01:10:06,510
I think I preferred the loin.

1614
01:10:06,710 --> 01:10:07,710
More than the short ribs?

1615
01:10:07,710 --> 01:10:09,210
No, the short ribs. This is the short ribs.

1616
01:10:09,210 --> 01:10:10,210
That's the short ribs.

1617
01:10:10,210 --> 01:10:12,850
Personally, I think short ribs are the tastiest cut of beef.

1618
01:10:13,450 --> 01:10:16,480
Loin and tenderloin are good too, but short ribs are the best.

1619
01:10:16,480 --> 01:10:18,350
- Right.
- Just the right amount...

1620
01:10:18,350 --> 01:10:19,990
- of fat.
- It's fatty.

1621
01:10:20,620 --> 01:10:22,690
- You don't like chicken feet?
- I don't.

1622
01:10:22,990 --> 01:10:24,660
- You don't like chicken feet?
- I really hate them.

1623
01:10:24,660 --> 01:10:26,630
- I hate them too.
- You too? That's amazing.

1624
01:10:26,630 --> 01:10:29,560
Back in Vietnam, I thought Hyun Moo and I really clicked.

1625
01:10:29,560 --> 01:10:30,560
But all three of us are alike.

1626
01:10:30,560 --> 01:10:31,970
Another person we click with has joined us.

1627
01:10:31,970 --> 01:10:33,330
It's really amazing.

1628
01:10:33,330 --> 01:10:34,770
We're like hermaphrodites.

1629
01:10:34,770 --> 01:10:36,270
(Three brothers, a perfect match)

1630
01:10:36,770 --> 01:10:39,210
Personally, I'd love for Nam Gil to become a regular.

1631
01:10:41,210 --> 01:10:42,210
Me too.

1632
01:10:42,210 --> 01:10:44,180
We clicked far more than I expected.

1633
01:10:44,310 --> 01:10:47,310
We have over 80 percent in common.

1634
01:10:47,310 --> 01:10:49,120
I think we really clicked and felt at ease together.

1635
01:10:49,750 --> 01:10:52,520
It's only been a day with Nam Gil,

1636
01:10:52,520 --> 01:10:56,320
and he's far more senior than I am as an actor...

1637
01:10:56,890 --> 01:11:00,160
- I feel intimidated by him.
- Of course.

1638
01:11:00,160 --> 01:11:03,460
I expected him to be charismatic and imposing,

1639
01:11:03,460 --> 01:11:05,300
but he's like an older guy from the neighbourhood.

1640
01:11:05,300 --> 01:11:07,070
- I'm not like that.
- So it's really...

1641
01:11:07,070 --> 01:11:09,970
surprising how different he is from what I expected.

1642
01:11:09,970 --> 01:11:12,570
He's nothing like I imagined.

1643
01:11:13,170 --> 01:11:14,610
Have some of this, Joon.

1644
01:11:14,910 --> 01:11:19,150
(An extra order of premium short ribs arrives.)

1645
01:11:20,080 --> 01:11:21,350
Oh, I'm full.

1646
01:11:21,350 --> 01:11:23,120
(Hyun Moo, the oldest, is starting to get full.)

1647
01:11:28,290 --> 01:11:29,420
It's delicious.

1648
01:11:32,290 --> 01:11:34,160
After your fan meetings in Japan,

1649
01:11:35,000 --> 01:11:37,670
you must have gone somewhere nearby for a nice meal with the team.

1650
01:11:37,670 --> 01:11:39,800
They end so late that no good places are open.

1651
01:11:39,800 --> 01:11:40,800
You don't eat afterward?

1652
01:11:40,800 --> 01:11:43,270
I do, but it's usually just a rice bowl at best.

1653
01:11:43,370 --> 01:11:44,370
What?

1654
01:11:44,370 --> 01:11:45,510
(He often makes do with rice bowls from chain restaurants.)

1655
01:11:45,510 --> 01:11:46,510
What are you talking about?

1656
01:11:46,510 --> 01:11:47,880
Lots of places are open at night.

1657
01:11:47,880 --> 01:11:49,380
But we just don't end up doing that.

1658
01:11:49,380 --> 01:11:51,480
- We're tired after finishing late.
- You're tired?

1659
01:11:51,480 --> 01:11:53,050
You talk for five hours. That's why.

1660
01:11:53,050 --> 01:11:54,280
(Five-hour fan meetings made him lose his appetite.)

1661
01:11:54,280 --> 01:11:55,820
- You're right.
- Talking for five hours...

1662
01:11:55,820 --> 01:11:57,690
- makes you lose your appetite.
- Exactly. It does.

1663
01:11:57,690 --> 01:11:59,320
At MBC,

1664
01:11:59,320 --> 01:12:02,120
I also held a fan meeting in the radio auditorium.

1665
01:12:02,120 --> 01:12:04,590
Oh, right. I saw you doing that.

1666
01:12:04,590 --> 01:12:05,860
- You saw it, right?
- Yes.

1667
01:12:05,860 --> 01:12:07,660
I posted the notice three hours beforehand.

1668
01:12:07,660 --> 01:12:08,700
So it was a surprise event.

1669
01:12:08,700 --> 01:12:10,360
With just three hours' notice,

1670
01:12:12,200 --> 01:12:13,370
we got 50 people.

1671
01:12:14,940 --> 01:12:18,110
(A sparsely filled auditorium)

1672
01:12:18,310 --> 01:12:20,410
(Oh dear.)

1673
01:12:20,810 --> 01:12:22,780
- Fifty people came?
- So many empty seats.

1674
01:12:23,080 --> 01:12:25,710
As soon as I stepped out,

1675
01:12:25,710 --> 01:12:27,980
I could see so many empty seats.

1676
01:12:28,380 --> 01:12:29,920
But it was a surprise event.

1677
01:12:29,920 --> 01:12:32,950
Right. For a surprise event, that turnout was impressive.

1678
01:12:32,950 --> 01:12:34,490
You gave just three hours' notice.

1679
01:12:34,490 --> 01:12:36,390
Couldn't you just keep it casual?

1680
01:12:36,420 --> 01:12:37,690
Since it was a surprise event.

1681
01:12:37,890 --> 01:12:40,260
It was on the website much earlier.

1682
01:12:40,260 --> 01:12:41,360
Oh, I see.

1683
01:12:41,960 --> 01:12:43,130
I'm not an idol.

1684
01:12:43,130 --> 01:12:45,800
But compared to an idol, you have far...

1685
01:12:45,800 --> 01:12:47,940
greater name recognition and you're well-loved.

1686
01:12:47,940 --> 01:12:51,610
See, someone like me doesn't have diehard fans.

1687
01:12:51,970 --> 01:12:53,370
- Why not?
- I don't.

1688
01:12:53,640 --> 01:12:54,840
- So many...
- But in Korea,

1689
01:12:54,840 --> 01:12:56,240
- everyone knows who you are.
- Right.

1690
01:12:56,240 --> 01:12:57,280
Everyone knows who I am,

1691
01:12:57,280 --> 01:12:58,980
and nobody dislikes me.

1692
01:12:58,980 --> 01:13:00,310
Nobody dislikes you?

1693
01:13:02,350 --> 01:13:03,420
Plenty of people do.

1694
01:13:04,450 --> 01:13:05,490
(That struck a nerve with Hyun Moo, the oldest.)

1695
01:13:05,490 --> 01:13:07,250
- But why?
- What I mean is...

1696
01:13:07,250 --> 01:13:09,060
- What I mean is...
- I'm just saying.

1697
01:13:09,060 --> 01:13:10,420
I don't have diehard fans.

1698
01:13:11,130 --> 01:13:12,360
Still, there was...

1699
01:13:12,830 --> 01:13:15,360
a time I wondered if this was how idols felt.

1700
01:13:15,500 --> 01:13:16,700
When was that?

1701
01:13:16,930 --> 01:13:18,300
When I was a radio DJ.

1702
01:13:19,530 --> 01:13:20,600
Back then,

1703
01:13:21,740 --> 01:13:23,900
- we'd broadcast before a crowd.
- Right.

1704
01:13:24,470 --> 01:13:25,810
I had 600 people show up.

1705
01:13:26,410 --> 01:13:27,410
What?

1706
01:13:27,410 --> 01:13:29,640
They weren't fans of Jun Hyun Moo,

1707
01:13:29,640 --> 01:13:31,180
but more fans of DJ Moo.

1708
01:13:33,080 --> 01:13:34,720
I was so surprised.

1709
01:13:35,250 --> 01:13:37,680
There were 600 people gathered in front of that truck.

1710
01:13:37,680 --> 01:13:38,920
I was really taken aback.

1711
01:13:39,590 --> 01:13:40,690
At that point...

1712
01:13:42,360 --> 01:13:44,320
- I get so fired up.
- Of course you do.

1713
01:13:44,320 --> 01:13:46,330
That's why I go on for 5 hours and 40 minutes.

1714
01:13:47,390 --> 01:13:48,700
- I guess that explains it.
- Seriously.

1715
01:13:48,700 --> 01:13:50,960
- And since you're so grateful, - Yes.

1716
01:13:51,270 --> 01:13:53,170
- you want to do more for them.
- Me too.

1717
01:13:53,170 --> 01:13:55,140
After two hours on air,

1718
01:13:55,340 --> 01:13:56,740
I spent an hour posing for photos.

1719
01:13:56,970 --> 01:13:58,310
Right.

1720
01:13:58,570 --> 01:14:00,810
- To show your gratitude.
- That's why you do it.

1721
01:14:01,710 --> 01:14:03,780
That's why people love us.

1722
01:14:04,440 --> 01:14:05,750
Shall we settle the bill now?

1723
01:14:06,150 --> 01:14:07,480
- Yes.
- How much is the total?

1724
01:14:07,820 --> 01:14:08,980
The total?

1725
01:14:08,980 --> 01:14:10,350
- Oh, shall I bring the bill?
- Yes.

1726
01:14:10,350 --> 01:14:11,620
Just a moment, please.

1727
01:14:13,590 --> 01:14:15,620
I feel like trying the potato salad.

1728
01:14:15,920 --> 01:14:18,360
(The second-oldest still wants more.)

1729
01:14:18,890 --> 01:14:21,230
- After eating so much?
- I know.

1730
01:14:21,600 --> 01:14:23,300
But you really don't gain weight.

1731
01:14:23,560 --> 01:14:26,470
Could we have the potato salad too?

1732
01:14:26,470 --> 01:14:27,570
- One serving?
- Yes.

1733
01:14:27,840 --> 01:14:28,900
Thank you.

1734
01:14:28,900 --> 01:14:30,370
No... Thank you.

1735
01:14:30,670 --> 01:14:32,140
You really have an appetite.

1736
01:14:33,210 --> 01:14:34,240
It's delicious.

1737
01:14:34,240 --> 01:14:37,310
(Nam Gil eats as much as he talks.)

1738
01:14:37,710 --> 01:14:39,350
You're not having that rice, Joon?

1739
01:14:39,350 --> 01:14:40,910
- Oh, gosh. Seriously.
- What's gotten into you?

1740
01:14:40,910 --> 01:14:42,520
How hungry are you?

1741
01:14:42,520 --> 01:14:44,750
Did you suddenly get worms? What's going on?

1742
01:14:44,750 --> 01:14:46,690
- I'm hungry.
- Do you always eat this much?

1743
01:14:46,690 --> 01:14:48,060
- What's gotten into you?
- I usually eat a lot.

1744
01:14:48,190 --> 01:14:49,390
Are you still hungry?

1745
01:14:50,160 --> 01:14:52,590
(Nam Gil is still hungry even as he eats.)

1746
01:14:52,590 --> 01:14:54,660
You've had two beers, potato salad,

1747
01:14:54,660 --> 01:14:57,600
plus 3 bowls of rice, with 1 mixed into soup,

1748
01:14:57,600 --> 01:15:00,570
and 3 or 4 plates of meat.

1749
01:15:00,700 --> 01:15:03,040
It's like you're in your 20s. You eat a lot in your early 20s.

1750
01:15:03,040 --> 01:15:05,610
You must have a high basal metabolic rate.

1751
01:15:05,970 --> 01:15:08,140
(The bill arrives.)

1752
01:15:10,540 --> 01:15:11,910
How much is this?

1753
01:15:12,780 --> 01:15:13,910
How much is this?

1754
01:15:13,910 --> 01:15:15,980
(Hold on.)

1755
01:15:16,120 --> 01:15:18,220
What is it?

1756
01:15:18,850 --> 01:15:21,520
Let's see. 1, 10, 100, 1,000, 10,000, 100,000.

1757
01:15:22,560 --> 01:15:24,930
- A thousand dollars.
- Twelve hundred dollars? Wait.

1758
01:15:24,930 --> 01:15:26,230
(What?)

1759
01:15:26,360 --> 01:15:28,000
- There must be a mistake, right?
- Twelve hundred dollars?

1760
01:15:28,000 --> 01:15:29,300
Twelve hundred fifty dollars.

1761
01:15:29,300 --> 01:15:32,200
(The absurd amount makes the two laugh in disbelief.)

1762
01:15:32,300 --> 01:15:34,600
(The second-oldest can't laugh.)

1763
01:15:34,970 --> 01:15:36,670
It's not 120,000 yen, is it?

1764
01:15:36,770 --> 01:15:38,740
Isn't it 24,870 yen?

1765
01:15:38,740 --> 01:15:39,940
It's in the ten thousands, right?

1766
01:15:40,270 --> 01:15:42,340
This looks like a one to me.

1767
01:15:42,340 --> 01:15:43,510
So that's wrong?

1768
01:15:43,940 --> 01:15:45,350
It's not a one, right?

1769
01:15:46,280 --> 01:15:48,250
I thought it was 1,240 dollars.

1770
01:15:48,520 --> 01:15:49,880
That really startled me.

1771
01:15:50,080 --> 01:15:51,720
I thought the old man was ripping us off.

1772
01:15:52,490 --> 01:15:53,750
- Okay.
- Thank you.

1773
01:15:54,320 --> 01:15:55,560
No matter how good the food was...

1774
01:15:55,560 --> 01:15:57,620
- I was shocked eating potato salad.
- Seriously.

1775
01:15:58,360 --> 01:16:00,090
(They soothe their nerves with potato salad.)

1776
01:16:06,100 --> 01:16:09,470
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

1777
01:16:09,740 --> 01:16:10,870
That really startled me.

1778
01:16:11,240 --> 01:16:13,410
- I'm so full.
- I'm so full.

1779
01:16:13,670 --> 01:16:16,810
(Time to head back to the house)

1780
01:16:17,180 --> 01:16:18,610
Are we going back to the house?

1781
01:16:18,610 --> 01:16:21,050
(The small town has quieted down for the night.)

1782
01:16:22,120 --> 01:16:23,920
Kim Nam Gil got the ball rolling.

1783
01:16:24,380 --> 01:16:25,520
McDonald's.

1784
01:16:26,050 --> 01:16:27,150
(McDonald's instead of the house?)

1785
01:16:27,150 --> 01:16:28,290
Where did you hear that?

1786
01:16:28,420 --> 01:16:29,720
You really won't regret this.

1787
01:16:31,360 --> 01:16:34,160
(It all started...)

1788
01:16:34,730 --> 01:16:36,760
(on the way to dinner.)

1789
01:16:36,760 --> 01:16:37,760
- Why?
- Why?

1790
01:16:37,760 --> 01:16:39,870
- In Japan, you must try this.
- You startled me.

1791
01:16:39,870 --> 01:16:40,870
- What?
- What?

1792
01:16:40,870 --> 01:16:43,270
You have to get the dessert at a fast-food shop.

1793
01:16:43,470 --> 01:16:44,740
- Why?
- I thought it was a big deal.

1794
01:16:44,740 --> 01:16:45,740
Why?

1795
01:16:45,740 --> 01:16:47,470
It's different from the one in Korea.

1796
01:16:47,640 --> 01:16:50,580
The one in Japan is really delicious.

1797
01:16:50,740 --> 01:16:51,750
Oh, gosh.

1798
01:16:51,980 --> 01:16:53,450
Vanilla ice cream.

1799
01:16:53,450 --> 01:16:54,480
It really is different.

1800
01:16:55,320 --> 01:16:57,620
It'll be different.

1801
01:16:57,620 --> 01:16:58,720
(Off to the fast-food shop to grant Nam Gil's wish)

1802
01:16:59,050 --> 01:17:01,390
This had better be special.

1803
01:17:01,590 --> 01:17:02,920
It'll be different.

1804
01:17:04,420 --> 01:17:06,090
Do I need to tell them beforehand?

1805
01:17:06,430 --> 01:17:07,660
A big serving, please.

1806
01:17:08,600 --> 01:17:10,360
Is that the only one you've tried?

1807
01:17:10,360 --> 01:17:13,570
It was delicious. It's the best.

1808
01:17:15,440 --> 01:17:16,740
I really do still...

1809
01:17:18,210 --> 01:17:19,270
like it.

1810
01:17:21,880 --> 01:17:23,280
Here we are.

1811
01:17:23,610 --> 01:17:26,850
I never thought an adult could love this place this much.

1812
01:17:27,280 --> 01:17:29,380
(The second-oldest is happy just thinking about a dessert.)

1813
01:17:30,380 --> 01:17:31,650
(At the fast-food shop in Japan)

1814
01:17:31,650 --> 01:17:33,950
(The ice-cream dessert is a must-try.)

1815
01:17:34,150 --> 01:17:36,160
(With a range of collab items)

1816
01:17:36,160 --> 01:17:38,030
(Fast-food shop in Japan)

1817
01:17:38,430 --> 01:17:39,590
(A long time favourite among locals)

1818
01:17:39,590 --> 01:17:40,830
(They chose to try the ice cream with chocolate cookie.)

1819
01:17:40,830 --> 01:17:42,900
Try it. See if it really tastes better than in Korea.

1820
01:17:46,430 --> 01:17:49,000
(It's just the taste Nam Gil was looking forward to.)

1821
01:17:49,500 --> 01:17:51,100
It does have a lot of cookie bits.

1822
01:17:51,270 --> 01:17:52,370
Let me try it.

1823
01:17:57,610 --> 01:17:59,980
- Lots of cookies in it, right?
- A bit more than in Korea.

1824
01:18:00,480 --> 01:18:01,680
It is good, though.

1825
01:18:03,350 --> 01:18:04,380
It's different.

1826
01:18:04,920 --> 01:18:07,350
(He's happy they approve,)

1827
01:18:08,690 --> 01:18:13,090
(but the second-oldest is happier just eating his dessert.)

1828
01:18:13,460 --> 01:18:14,730
Oh, they're bringing it to us.

1829
01:18:14,730 --> 01:18:15,930
(Hyun Moo's secret order arrives.)

1830
01:18:15,930 --> 01:18:17,230
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

1831
01:18:17,230 --> 01:18:18,330
What is it?

1832
01:18:20,070 --> 01:18:22,170
There's a Japanese soda called Ramune.

1833
01:18:23,170 --> 01:18:24,810
They make a shake with it. You can only get here.

1834
01:18:30,040 --> 01:18:31,780
How is it?

1835
01:18:31,910 --> 01:18:33,910
It's just the kind of flavour an elementary school kid would like.

1836
01:18:34,680 --> 01:18:36,550
- Lemon-lime soda flavour.
- This...

1837
01:18:37,080 --> 01:18:38,750
Kids would really love this.

1838
01:18:38,750 --> 01:18:41,320
(Kids would really love this.)

1839
01:18:48,960 --> 01:18:50,730
- You can only get this here?
- Yes.

1840
01:18:51,260 --> 01:18:52,570
Since it's a Japanese soda.

1841
01:18:53,600 --> 01:18:55,640
(Oh, yes.)

1842
01:18:55,640 --> 01:18:56,770
At each chain,

1843
01:18:56,770 --> 01:18:58,570
it's fun to try foods that are only available locally.

1844
01:18:58,640 --> 01:19:01,440
(See you next time.)

1845
01:19:01,440 --> 01:19:02,810
- I enjoyed it.
- I enjoyed it.

1846
01:19:03,780 --> 01:19:04,810
I enjoyed it.

1847
01:19:07,850 --> 01:19:10,280
(Perfect for savouring the memories of the trip)

1848
01:19:10,280 --> 01:19:14,350
(A refreshing summer night)

1849
01:19:20,330 --> 01:19:22,330
Aren't you going to wash up? You should wash before bed.

1850
01:19:23,730 --> 01:19:26,730
(Hyun Moo, the oldest, is drifting in and out of sleep.)

1851
01:19:27,070 --> 01:19:28,170
Come wash up already.

1852
01:19:29,170 --> 01:19:30,300
In a bit.

1853
01:19:31,570 --> 01:19:34,210
I don't think you'll manage if you leave it till later.

1854
01:19:34,410 --> 01:19:35,740
But I'm so sleepy.

1855
01:19:40,410 --> 01:19:41,720
(Reading the room)

1856
01:19:42,120 --> 01:19:43,150
Why?

1857
01:19:44,420 --> 01:19:45,690
I didn't bring any pants.

1858
01:19:46,820 --> 01:19:48,860
So you wore those pants again?

1859
01:19:49,620 --> 01:19:50,890
(Joon, the one-outfit man)

1860
01:19:51,590 --> 01:19:53,960
(Sighing)

1861
01:19:54,730 --> 01:19:56,460
What should we do? Do you want to go to sleep now?

1862
01:19:57,130 --> 01:19:58,570
No, you're supposed to talk here.

1863
01:19:58,770 --> 01:20:00,170
Think of it as a hospital visit.

1864
01:20:01,300 --> 01:20:03,270
- Really?
- Talk here.

1865
01:20:03,670 --> 01:20:05,310
- No...
- A hospital visit...

1866
01:20:05,310 --> 01:20:06,940
Over a glass of draft beer tonight,

1867
01:20:06,940 --> 01:20:08,410
I really want to hear Joon's story.

1868
01:20:08,580 --> 01:20:09,680
If you're okay with it, Joon,

1869
01:20:09,680 --> 01:20:11,410
will you give me a chance to listen too?

1870
01:20:11,510 --> 01:20:13,310
If you listen to Joon's story,

1871
01:20:13,510 --> 01:20:14,880
you have to promise me one thing.

1872
01:20:15,380 --> 01:20:17,180
You must promise not to get angry.

1873
01:20:18,890 --> 01:20:20,120
All right, tell me.

1874
01:20:20,520 --> 01:20:23,860
But there's so much build up...

1875
01:20:23,860 --> 01:20:26,060
The expectation is high.

1876
01:20:26,060 --> 01:20:28,130
Look how even he opened his eyes when we mentioned the story.

1877
01:20:29,760 --> 01:20:33,070
What was that all about, bringing it up so suddenly earlier?

1878
01:20:33,730 --> 01:20:36,670
Wait, isn't that camera on?

1879
01:20:36,670 --> 01:20:38,710
Turn it off. Press the left one.

1880
01:20:39,070 --> 01:20:41,070
- This one?
- See the one sticking up?

1881
01:20:41,070 --> 01:20:42,110
This one?

1882
01:20:42,110 --> 01:20:44,040
(They even turn the camera off for a deep conversation.)

1883
01:20:44,340 --> 01:20:45,510
(Laughter)

1884
01:20:45,510 --> 01:20:46,850
So then...

1885
01:20:47,610 --> 01:20:48,780
I'm tearing up.

1886
01:20:50,120 --> 01:20:54,960
(Travel itinerary available on our official social media account)

1887
01:20:55,360 --> 01:20:58,390
(Day two in Tokushima)

1888
01:20:58,660 --> 01:21:00,090
(The trio's all-in-one trip continues.)

1889
01:21:00,090 --> 01:21:01,660
(Having fun up in the sky too)

1890
01:21:01,660 --> 01:21:03,000
(Activities are more fun together.)

1891
01:21:03,360 --> 01:21:05,430
(Drives are more fun together.)

1892
01:21:05,530 --> 01:21:07,300
(Walking together)

1893
01:21:07,900 --> 01:21:10,270
(Tasting together)

1894
01:21:10,640 --> 01:21:12,670
(The trio's all in together trip)

1895
01:21:13,840 --> 01:21:16,580
(The more time they share, the closer the trio grows.)

1896
01:21:16,840 --> 01:21:18,410
(And...)

1897
01:21:18,410 --> 01:21:20,310
(Getting closer reveals new sides of the second-oldest.)

1898
01:21:21,280 --> 01:21:23,720
(The more you know him, the more you fall for his charm.)

1899
01:21:24,150 --> 01:21:25,720
(The second-oldest's charm grows with his brothers' love.)

1900
01:21:25,920 --> 01:21:27,090
It's "Would you marry me?"

1901
01:21:27,090 --> 01:21:29,420
(The second-oldest really is all in one.)

1902
01:21:30,790 --> 01:21:32,130
(But...)

1903
01:21:32,360 --> 01:21:35,400
(The second-oldest heads off somewhere alone.)

1904
01:21:36,530 --> 01:21:38,060
- Where did he go?
- Hey!

1905
01:21:38,160 --> 01:21:39,300
(Is that Nam Gil screaming?)

1906
01:21:39,830 --> 01:21:40,830
(We have to save the second-oldest.)

1907
01:21:41,130 --> 01:21:42,170
(Isn't this a bit scary?)

1908
01:21:42,300 --> 01:21:43,870
(Even the entrance feels ominous.)

1909
01:21:44,970 --> 01:21:46,640
(Mysterious objects?)

1910
01:21:47,440 --> 01:21:48,440
Oh, gosh.

1911
01:21:48,440 --> 01:21:49,940
(I really got goosebumps.)

1912
01:21:50,240 --> 01:21:52,750
(What's happening to the trio?)
